                        {
                            "qNum": "Q5",
                            "globalIndex": 14,
                            "q": "<p>We can also find the gcd of two numbers by computing prime factorisation of both the numbers. Try to write an algorithm to compute the prime factorisation of a number.<br>\n          (i) The prime factorisation of 180 is $2^2 \\times 3^2 \\times 5^1$. How would you represent this?<br>\n          (ii) How would you compare the prime factorisations of two numbers?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>Algorithm for Prime Factorisation of n:</strong><br>\n          1. Create an empty list called 'factors'.<br>\n          2. Set $d = 2$ (the smallest prime).<br>\n          3. While $n > 1$:<br>\n          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;If $n$ is divisible by $d$:<br>\n          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Add $d$ to 'factors'.<br>\n          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Set $n = n / d$.<br>\n          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Else:<br>\n          &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Increase $d$ by 1.<br>\n          4. Report 'factors'.<br><br>\n          <strong>(i) Representation:</strong> It can be represented as a list of base-exponent pairs (e.g., [(2, 2), (3, 2), (5, 1)]) or just a flat list of primes [2, 2, 3, 3, 5].<br>\n          <strong>(ii) Comparing factorisations to find GCD:</strong> To find the GCD, identify the prime bases that appear in <strong>both</strong> factorisations. For each common prime base, take the <strong>minimum</strong> of the exponents it has in the two representations. Multiply these resulting prime powers together to get the GCD.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
