                {
                    "name": "Chapter 12: Quadrilaterals",
                    "exercises": [
                        {
                            "id": "12.1",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 0,
                                    "q": "<p>Let ABCD be a quadrilateral.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>(i) List all sides of ABCD adjacent to side AB. List all sides opposite to AB.</li>\n    <li>(ii) List all angles of ABCD adjacent to &angle;A. List all angles opposite to &angle;A.</li>\n    <li>(iii) Define a pair of opposite sides and a pair of opposite angles without using the names of the vertices.</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i)</strong> Adjacent to AB: Sides <strong>BC</strong> and <strong>DA</strong>. Opposite to AB: Side <strong>CD</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii)</strong> Adjacent to &angle;A: Angles <strong>&angle;B</strong> and <strong>&angle;D</strong>. Opposite to &angle;A: Angle <strong>&angle;C</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>(iii)</strong> <strong>Opposite sides</strong> are two sides of a quadrilateral that do not share a common vertex. <strong>Opposite angles</strong> are two internal angles that do not share a common side.</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 1,
                                    "q": "<p>Precisely define the internal angle of a quadrilateral at a given vertex. Your answer should work for a non-convex quadrilateral too.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> At any vertex of a quadrilateral, the two adjacent sides form an angle. The <strong>internal angle</strong> is the angle formed by these two rays that sweeps across the interior region of the quadrilateral. To define it precisely without ambiguity (especially for non-convex cases): It is the angle between the two adjacent sides at the vertex such that a line segment connecting points immediately near the vertex on these two sides lies completely within the quadrilateral's interior. If the quadrilateral is non-convex (has a \"dent\"), this interior angle will be greater than 180&deg;.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"340\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 165\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Convex Quad -->\n        <polygon points=\"30,130 55,35 135,45 145,130\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.1)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"90\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0369a1\">Convex (All angles &lt; 180°)</text>\n        <!-- Non-Convex (Dart) Quad -->\n        <polygon points=\"195,130 255,25 315,130 255,85\" fill=\"rgba(225,29,72,0.1)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <path d=\"M 241 95 A 18 18 0 1 1 269 95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" />\n        <text x=\"255\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">&gt; 180°</text>\n        <text x=\"255\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Non-Convex (Reflex interior angle)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Convex vs. Non-Convex quadrilateral showing internal reflex angle > 180°</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 2,
                                    "q": "<p>In a quadrilateral ABCD, suppose AB || DC. Can ABCD be non-convex? What if we instead assume AB = CD? What if we instead assume &angle;A = &angle;C?</p>",
                                    "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>If AB || DC:</strong> No, it cannot be non-convex. If two opposite sides are parallel, the consecutive interior angles must sum to 180&deg; (e.g., &angle;A + &angle;D = 180&deg;). Since their sum is 180&deg;, neither angle can be greater than 180&deg;. Thus, it must be convex.</li>\n    <li><strong>If AB = CD:</strong> Yes, it can be non-convex. A self-intersecting \"crossed\" quadrilateral or a non-convex dart shape can have a pair of opposite sides equal.</li>\n    <li><strong>If &angle;A = &angle;C:</strong> Yes, it can be non-convex. A dart shape (non-convex quadrilateral) can be symmetric such that its two non-reflex opposite angles are equal.</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 3,
                                    "q": "<p>Consider three non-collinear points A, B, C and draw the lines AB, BC, CA. For every possible location of point D in the plane outside these lines, decide if ABCD is self-intersecting, non-convex, or convex.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The three lines extending from the triangle ABC divide the plane into 7 regions (the central triangle, and 6 outer regions). Depending on where point D is placed, the resulting sequence of vertices A-B-C-D forms different types of quadrilaterals.</p>\n<svg width=\"250\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">B</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">C</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">A</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"125\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"gray\">Convex</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"gray\">Self-Int.</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"110\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"gray\">Non-convex</text>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n    <li>If D is placed in the regions directly \"behind\" the vertices (e.g., opposite the triangle from A, B, or C), ABCD will be a <strong>self-intersecting</strong> (crossed) quadrilateral.</li>\n    <li>If D is placed in the regions bounded by the extended sides (the \"wedges\" pointing outwards from the edges), ABCD will be <strong>non-convex</strong> (dart-shaped).</li>\n    <li>If D is placed such that all internal angles remain less than 180&deg;, it forms a <strong>convex</strong> quadrilateral.</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "12.2",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 4,
                                    "q": "<p><strong>1. True or false?</strong></p>\n<ol>\n    <li>(i) A parallelogram with a right angle is a rectangle.</li>\n    <li>(ii) A rhombus with perpendicular diagonals is a square.</li>\n    <li>(iii) If the diagonals of a parallelogram are equal, then it is a rectangle.</li>\n</ol>",
                                    "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i) True.</strong> If one angle is 90&deg;, the opposite angle is also 90&deg;. The adjacent angles must sum to 180&deg;, making them 90&deg; as well. A quadrilateral with four right angles is a rectangle.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii) False.</strong> <em>Every</em> rhombus has perpendicular diagonals. It only becomes a square if its diagonals are also equal in length (or if it has a right angle).</li>\n    <li><strong>(iii) True.</strong> A parallelogram with equal diagonals guarantees that all four angles are 90&deg;, satisfying the definition of a rectangle.</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 5,
                                    "q": "<p>The diagonal AC of a parallelogram ABCD bisects &angle;A. Show that it also bisects &angle;C and that ABCD is a rhombus.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. Since ABCD is a parallelogram, AB || DC and AD || BC.<br>\n2. Given AC bisects &angle;A, we have &angle;DAC = &angle;BAC.<br>\n3. Because AB || DC, the alternate interior angles are equal: &angle;BAC = &angle;DCA.<br>\n4. Similarly, because AD || BC, the alternate interior angles are equal: &angle;DAC = &angle;BCA.<br>\n5. By substitution, &angle;DCA = &angle;BCA. Thus, AC also bisects &angle;C.<br>\n6. In &Delta;ADC, since &angle;DAC = &angle;DCA, the sides opposite to equal angles are equal: AD = DC.<br>\n7. Since adjacent sides of the parallelogram are equal, all four sides must be equal (AB = DC = AD = BC). Therefore, ABCD is a rhombus.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"175\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 175\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Rhombus ABCD: A=(45,140), B=(165,140), C=(210,35), D=(90,35) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,140 165,140 210,35 90,35\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"30\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"172\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"216\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"73\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">D</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Diagonal AC bisects ∠A and ∠C ⇒ AD = DC ⇒ ABCD is a Rhombus</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 6,
                                    "q": "<p>The following questions examine converses of true properties. Answer them with Yes or No. If your answer is No, what extra condition can you add so that the answer becomes Yes?</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>(i) If the diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD bisect its angles, must ABCD be a rhombus?</li>\n    <li>(ii) If the diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD bisect each other at right angles, must ABCD be a rhombus?</li>\n    <li>(iii) If the diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD are of equal length, must ABCD be a rectangle?</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i) Yes.</strong> If diagonals bisect the angles, you can prove through triangle congruence (ASA) that adjacent sides are equal, making it a rhombus.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii) Yes.</strong> If the diagonals bisect each other, it is guaranteed to be a parallelogram. If they do so at right angles, it guarantees all four sides are equal, making it a rhombus.</li>\n    <li><strong>(iii) No.</strong> An isosceles trapezium also has equal diagonals but is not a rectangle. <strong>Extra condition:</strong> Add \"If the diagonals <em>bisect each other</em> and are of equal length...\" then it becomes a rectangle.</li>\n</ul>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 7,
                                    "q": "<p>Let ABCD be a parallelogram with AB &ne; BC. Show that the pairwise intersection points of the four angle bisectors form the vertices of a rectangle. Why did we assume AB &ne; BC?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\nLet the bisectors of &angle;A and &angle;B intersect at P, &angle;B and &angle;C intersect at Q, &angle;C and &angle;D intersect at R, and &angle;D and &angle;A intersect at S.<br>\n1. In a parallelogram, consecutive angles sum to 180&deg;. Therefore, &angle;A + &angle;D = 180&deg;.<br>\n2. The bisectors of A and D form &Delta;ASD. &angle;DAS + &angle;ADS = (&angle;A + &angle;D)/2 = 180&deg;/2 = 90&deg;.<br>\n3. The third angle of the triangle, &angle;ASD, must be 180&deg; - 90&deg; = 90&deg;.<br>\n4. By the same logic, the intersections at P, Q, and R also form 90&deg; angles. A quadrilateral PQRS with four right angles is a rectangle.<br>\n<strong>Why assume AB &ne; BC?</strong> If AB = BC, the parallelogram is a rhombus. In a rhombus, the angle bisectors are the diagonals themselves, meaning they intersect at exactly one point rather than forming a new four-sided region.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"290\" height=\"180\" viewBox=\"0 0 290 180\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Parallelogram ABCD: A=(35,145), B=(215,145), C=(255,35), D=(75,35) -->\n        <polygon points=\"35,145 215,145 255,35 75,35\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Inner rectangle PQRS formed by angle bisectors -->\n        <polygon points=\"125,112 162,75 165,68 128,105\" fill=\"none\" />\n        <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <line x1=\"75\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <line x1=\"255\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <line x1=\"215\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"116,93 145,67 174,87 145,113\" fill=\"rgba(225,29,72,0.18)\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"262\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"58\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"145\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#be123c\">PQRS (90°)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Pairwise intersections of the four angle bisectors of a parallelogram form rectangle PQRS</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "12.3",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 8,
                                    "q": "<p>(i) If P, Q, R are the midpoints of sides AB, AC, BC respectively of &Delta;ABC, show that &Delta;PQR is congruent to &Delta;QPA and to two other triangles which you should identify.<br>\n(ii) Suppose someone erases &Delta;ABC, leaving only &Delta;PQR on the paper. Can you reconstruct &Delta;ABC from &Delta;PQR?</p>",
                                    "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i)</strong> By the Midpoint Theorem, PQ || BC, QR || AB, and RP || AC. Because PQ || BR and PR || BQ, PBQR is a parallelogram. The diagonal PR divides it into two congruent triangles: &Delta;PQR &cong; &Delta;RBP. By the same logic for the other parallelograms, &Delta;PQR &cong; &Delta;QPA &cong; &Delta;CQR.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii) Yes, you can reconstruct &Delta;ABC.</strong> To find the original vertices A, B, and C, simply draw a line through point P parallel to QR, a line through Q parallel to PR, and a line through R parallel to PQ. The intersections of these three lines will perfectly recreate the original &Delta;ABC.</li>\n</ul>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"250\" height=\"190\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 190\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Triangle ABC: A=(125, 25), B=(35, 165), C=(215, 165) -->\n        <!-- Midpoints: P=(80, 95) on AB, Q=(170, 95) on AC, R=(125, 165) on BC -->\n        <polygon points=\"125,25 35,165 215,165\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"80,95 170,95 125,165\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"125\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"64\" y=\"96\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"176\" y=\"96\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">Q</text>\n        <text x=\"125\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">R</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Medial triangle ΔPQR divides ΔABC into 4 congruent triangles: ΔPQR ≅ ΔQPA ≅ ΔRBP ≅ ΔCQR</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 9,
                                    "q": "<p>In &Delta;ABC, let M and N be midpoints of AB and AC respectively. Let D be any point on BC. Show that MN bisects AD.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. By the Midpoint Theorem, MN connects the midpoints of AB and AC, therefore MN || BC.<br>\n2. Let the segment AD intersect MN at point X.<br>\n3. In &Delta;ABD, M is the midpoint of AB, and the segment MX is part of the line MN, which is parallel to the base BD (since MN || BC).<br>\n4. By the Converse of the Midpoint Theorem (Theorem 7), a line drawn through the midpoint of one side of a triangle parallel to the base will bisect the third side.<br>\n5. Therefore, MX bisects AD, meaning X is the midpoint of AD. Thus, MN bisects AD.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"250\" height=\"190\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 190\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <polygon points=\"115,25 35,165 215,165\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Midsegment MN: M=(75, 95), N=(165, 95) -->\n        <line x1=\"75\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Segment AD: A=(115,25) to D=(145,165), midpoint X=(130, 95) -->\n        <line x1=\"115\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"95\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"115\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"58\" y=\"98\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">M</text>\n        <text x=\"172\" y=\"98\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">N</text>\n        <text x=\"145\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"138\" y=\"88\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">X</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Midsegment MN ∥ BC bisects segment AD at X (AX = XD)</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 10,
                                    "q": "<p>Suppose the midpoints of sides AB, BC, CD and DA of a quadrilateral ABCD are P, Q, R and S respectively.<br>\n(i) Show that PR and QS bisect each other.<br>\n(ii) Show that if AC = BD, then PR and QS are perpendicular. Is the converse true?</p>",
                                    "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i)</strong> By Varignon's Theorem (Theorem 9), joining the midpoints of any quadrilateral forms a parallelogram. Thus, PQRS is a parallelogram. In any parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other. Therefore, PR and QS bisect each other.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii)</strong> If the diagonals of the original quadrilateral are equal (AC = BD), then the sides of the Varignon parallelogram PQRS, which are half the lengths of the diagonals (PQ = SR = AC/2 and PS = QR = BD/2), must be equal to each other. A parallelogram with equal adjacent sides is a rhombus. The diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular. Thus, PR &perp; QS. <strong>Yes, the converse is true</strong>: If PR &perp; QS, then PQRS is a rhombus, meaning its sides are equal, which forces AC = BD.</li>\n</ul>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 200\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Quad ABCD: A=(40,160), B=(210,170), C=(225,45), D=(70,30) -->\n        <!-- Midpoints: P=(125,165), Q=(217.5,107.5), R=(147.5,37.5), S=(55,95) -->\n        <polygon points=\"40,160 210,170 225,45 70,30\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"125,165 217.5,107.5 147.5,37.5 55,95\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Diagonals PR and QS of Varignon parallelogram -->\n        <line x1=\"125\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"147.5\" y2=\"37.5\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"217.5\" y1=\"107.5\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <text x=\"25\" y=\"168\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"216\" y=\"178\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"232\" y=\"45\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"55\" y=\"28\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"125\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"226\" y=\"112\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Q</text>\n        <text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">R</text>\n        <text x=\"38\" y=\"98\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">S</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Varignon Parallelogram PQRS: Diagonals PR and QS bisect each other</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "12.4",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 12.4",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 12.4",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 11,
                                    "q": "<p>Justify why the plane cannot be tiled with a regular pentagon.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Tiling the plane requires shapes to fit together around a vertex such that the sum of the angles meeting at that vertex equals exactly 360&deg;, leaving no gaps or overlaps.<br>\nThe interior angle of a regular pentagon is 108&deg;.<br>\nIf we try to fit regular pentagons around a vertex, three pentagons give 108&deg; &times; 3 = 324&deg;. This leaves a gap of 36&deg;. A fourth pentagon would make the sum 432&deg;, causing an overlap. Because 360 is not evenly divisible by 108, the plane cannot be tiled with regular pentagons.</p>\n<div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: center;\"><svg width=\"280\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 200\" style=\"background:#f8fafc; border:1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius:8px; max-width:100%;\">\n  <!-- Central vertex V at (140, 105) -->\n  <!-- Pentagon 1 (top-left) -->\n  <polygon points=\"140,105 90,105 75,57 115,28 155,57\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Pentagon 2 (bottom-left) -->\n  <polygon points=\"140,105 90,105 75,153 115,182 155,153\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Pentagon 3 (right, rotated by 108 deg from P1 edge (140,105)-(155,57)) -->\n  <polygon points=\"140,105 155,57 203,72 203,122 155,137\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- 36 degree gap wedge between (140,105)-(155,137) and (140,105)-(155,153) -->\n  <path d=\"M 140 105 L 175 180 M 140 105 L 195 162\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"105\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"112\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e40af\">108°</text>\n  <text x=\"112\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">108°</text>\n  <text x=\"162\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#15803d\">108°</text>\n  <text x=\"208\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\">36° Gap!</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">3 × 108° = 324° &lt; 360° (Cannot Tile)</text>\n</svg><div style=\"font-size: 0.85em; color: #64748b; margin-top: 4px;\">Three Regular Pentagons at a Vertex Leave a 36° Uncovered Gap (4 × 108° = 432° > 360° Overlaps)</div></div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "12.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "end-of-chapter",
                            "name": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "title": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 12,
                                    "q": "<p>Using a fact about parallelograms, show how to tile the plane using any given triangle.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n1. Take any given triangle and create an exact duplicate of it.<br>\n2. Rotate the duplicate by 180&deg; and align it along one of the sides of the original triangle.<br>\n3. The two triangles together form a parallelogram. (Because alternate interior angles are equal, making opposite sides parallel).<br>\n4. As discussed at the beginning of the chapter, the plane can always be tiled using copies of any fixed parallelogram by creating a grid of continuous parallel lines. Therefore, by tiling parallelograms, we successfully tile the plane using the given triangle.</p>\n<svg width=\"200\" height=\"100\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <polygon points=\"10,90 90,90 50,10\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <polygon points=\"90,90 130,10 50,10\" fill=\"lightgreen\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n</svg>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 13,
                                    "q": "<p>You know that the sum of angles of a quadrilateral is 360&deg;, even for a non-convex quadrilateral. Now consider a self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD, where AB and CD intersect at point E. Show that &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D < 360&deg;.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. A self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD with intersecting sides AB and CD at point E forms two distinct triangles: &Delta;ADE and &Delta;BCE.<br>\n2. The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is 180&deg;. So, for &Delta;ADE: &angle;A + &angle;D + &angle;AED = 180&deg;.<br>\n3. For &Delta;BCE: &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;BEC = 180&deg;.<br>\n4. Adding both equations together: &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D + &angle;AED + &angle;BEC = 360&deg;.<br>\n5. Angles &angle;AED and &angle;BEC are vertically opposite angles at intersection E, and they have a positive measure greater than 0&deg;.<br>\n6. Therefore, &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D = 360&deg; - (&angle;AED + &angle;BEC). Since we are subtracting a positive value from 360&deg;, it proves that &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D < 360&deg;.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Crossed quad ABCD where AB and CD intersect at E(130, 95) -->\n        <!-- A=(45, 40), D=(45, 150), B=(215, 150), C=(215, 40) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,40 130,95 45,150\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"215,150 130,95 215,40\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"95\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"28\" y=\"42\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"28\" y=\"155\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"155\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"42\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"130\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">E</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD forming ΔADE and ΔBCE: ∠A + ∠B + ∠C + ∠D < 360°</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 14,
                                    "q": "<p>In a parallelogram ABCD, two points P and Q are taken on diagonal BD such that DP = BQ. Show that APCQ is a parallelogram.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. Let the diagonals AC and BD of the original parallelogram ABCD intersect at point O.<br>\n2. We know that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. Therefore, OA = OC and OD = OB.<br>\n3. It is given that DP = BQ. <br>\n4. Let's find the distances from the center O to points P and Q: <br>\n   OP = OD - DP<br>\n   OQ = OB - BQ<br>\n5. Since OD = OB and DP = BQ, it must be true that OP = OQ.<br>\n6. In the inner quadrilateral APCQ, the diagonals are AC and PQ. We already know OA = OC, and we just proved OP = OQ.<br>\n7. Since the diagonals of APCQ bisect each other at O, by the converse of the diagonal property (Theorem 4), APCQ is a parallelogram.</p>\n<svg width=\"250\" height=\"150\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <polygon points=\"50,120 200,120 230,30 80,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <polygon points=\"50,120 120,90 230,30 160,60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"blue\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"130\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">A</text>\n  <text x=\"205\" y=\"135\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">B</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">C</text>\n  <text x=\"65\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">D</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">P</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"75\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">Q</text>\n</svg>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q8",
                                    "globalIndex": 15,
                                    "q": "<p>The diagonals AC and BD of a parallelogram ABCD intersect at O. A line through O meets AB and CD at points P and Q respectively. Show that O is the midpoint of PQ.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. Because ABCD is a parallelogram, AB || DC.<br>\n2. Consider the triangles &Delta;APO and &Delta;CQO formed by the line passing through O.<br>\n3. The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so OA = OC.<br>\n4. Because AB || DC, the alternate interior angles formed by transversal AC are equal: &angle;PAO = &angle;QCO.<br>\n5. The vertically opposite angles at O are equal: &angle;AOP = &angle;COQ.<br>\n6. By Angle-Side-Angle (ASA) congruence, &Delta;APO &cong; &Delta;CQO.<br>\n7. Therefore, by Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles (CPCTC), OP = OQ. Thus, O is the midpoint of PQ.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"270\" height=\"180\" viewBox=\"0 0 270 180\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Parallelogram ABCD: A=(40,145), B=(200,145), C=(230,35), D=(70,35); Centre O=(135,90) -->\n        <polygon points=\"40,145 200,145 230,35 70,35\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <!-- Line PQ through O(135,90): P=(115,145) on AB, Q=(155,35) on CD -->\n        <line x1=\"115\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n        <circle cx=\"135\" cy=\"90\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#1e293b\" />\n        <text x=\"25\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"206\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"236\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"55\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"115\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"155\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Q</text>\n        <text x=\"148\" y=\"95\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">O</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">ΔAPO ≅ ΔCQO (by ASA) ⇒ OP = OQ</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q12",
                                    "globalIndex": 16,
                                    "q": "<p>Suppose ABCD is a parallelogram and M, N are midpoints of AB and CD respectively. Show that segments DM and BN trisect segment AC.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. ABCD is a parallelogram, so AB || DC and AB = DC.<br>\n2. M and N are midpoints, so AM = MB = 1/2 AB, and DN = NC = 1/2 DC.<br>\n3. Because AB = DC, it implies AM = NC and MB = DN. Also, MB || DN.<br>\n4. A quadrilateral with one pair of equal and parallel opposite sides is a parallelogram (Theorem 5). Thus, MBND is a parallelogram, which means DM || BN.<br>\n5. Let DM intersect AC at point P, and BN intersect AC at point Q.<br>\n6. In &Delta;ABQ, M is the midpoint of AB, and MP || BQ (since DM || BN). By the Converse of the Midpoint Theorem, P must be the midpoint of AQ. Therefore, AP = PQ.<br>\n7. In &Delta;CDP, N is the midpoint of CD, and NQ || DP (since BN || DM). By the Converse of the Midpoint Theorem, Q must be the midpoint of PC. Therefore, PQ = QC.<br>\n8. Combining these gives AP = PQ = QC. The points P and Q divide the diagonal AC into three equal parts, proving that DM and BN trisect AC.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"280\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Parallelogram ABCD: A=(35,145), B=(195,145), C=(245,35), D=(85,35) -->\n        <!-- Midpoints: M=(115,145) on AB, N=(165,35) on CD -->\n        <polygon points=\"35,145 195,145 245,35 85,35\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Trisection points P=(105, 108.3), Q=(175, 71.7) -->\n        <circle cx=\"105\" cy=\"108.3\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <circle cx=\"175\" cy=\"71.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"202\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"252\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"70\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"115\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">M</text>\n        <text x=\"165\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">N</text>\n        <text x=\"92\" y=\"102\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"182\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Q</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Parallel segments DM and BN trisect diagonal AC at P and Q (AP = PQ = QC)</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 4
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q17",
                                    "globalIndex": 17,
                                    "q": "<p>Show that the sum of angles of a non-planar quadrilateral is always less than 360&deg;.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\nThink of a non-planar (skew) quadrilateral ABCD folded along the diagonal AC like a hinge.<br>\n1. If the figure were completely flat (planar), the sum of the angles would be exactly 360&deg;.<br>\n2. When you fold the quadrilateral along AC to make it non-planar, the points B and D are brought closer together.<br>\n3. In 3D space, the straight-line distance between B and D becomes shorter. Because the side lengths AB, BC, CD, DA remain fixed, reducing the distance BD forces the angles at A (&angle;BAD) and C (&angle;BCD) to become strictly smaller (by the Law of Cosines for the hinge).<br>\n4. The angles at B (&angle;ABC) and D (&angle;ADC) remain unchanged because the triangles &Delta;ABC and &Delta;ADC themselves do not warp.<br>\n5. Since two interior angles shrink while the other two stay the same, the total sum of the four interior angles must strictly be less than 360&deg;.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 5
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q21",
                                    "globalIndex": 18,
                                    "q": "<p>Sum of angles of a polygon. What is the sum of angles of a (planar non-self-intersecting) n-gon? Find a formula in terms of n and prove it.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n1. The sum of the interior angles of a planar n-gon is given by the formula: <strong>180&deg; &times; (n - 2)</strong>.<br>\n2. <strong>Proof:</strong> Choose any single vertex of the n-gon. Draw diagonals from this chosen vertex to all other non-adjacent vertices.<br>\n3. Since you cannot draw a diagonal to the vertex itself, nor to the two immediately adjacent vertices, you will draw (n - 3) diagonals.<br>\n4. These (n - 3) diagonals perfectly divide the interior of the n-gon into exactly <strong>(n - 2) distinct triangles</strong>.<br>\n5. The sum of the interior angles of one triangle is 180&deg;. Because these triangles completely cover the n-gon without overlapping, the sum of all interior angles of the n-gon is simply the sum of the interior angles of these (n - 2) triangles.<br>\n6. Therefore, Sum = (n - 2) &times; 180&deg;.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 6
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ],
                    "solutions": [
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 0,
                            "q": "<p>Let ABCD be a quadrilateral.</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>(i) List all sides of ABCD adjacent to side AB. List all sides opposite to AB.</li>\n    <li>(ii) List all angles of ABCD adjacent to &angle;A. List all angles opposite to &angle;A.</li>\n    <li>(iii) Define a pair of opposite sides and a pair of opposite angles without using the names of the vertices.</li>\n</ul>",
                            "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i)</strong> Adjacent to AB: Sides <strong>BC</strong> and <strong>DA</strong>. Opposite to AB: Side <strong>CD</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii)</strong> Adjacent to &angle;A: Angles <strong>&angle;B</strong> and <strong>&angle;D</strong>. Opposite to &angle;A: Angle <strong>&angle;C</strong>.</li>\n    <li><strong>(iii)</strong> <strong>Opposite sides</strong> are two sides of a quadrilateral that do not share a common vertex. <strong>Opposite angles</strong> are two internal angles that do not share a common side.</li>\n</ul>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 1,
                            "q": "<p>Precisely define the internal angle of a quadrilateral at a given vertex. Your answer should work for a non-convex quadrilateral too.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> At any vertex of a quadrilateral, the two adjacent sides form an angle. The <strong>internal angle</strong> is the angle formed by these two rays that sweeps across the interior region of the quadrilateral. To define it precisely without ambiguity (especially for non-convex cases): It is the angle between the two adjacent sides at the vertex such that a line segment connecting points immediately near the vertex on these two sides lies completely within the quadrilateral's interior. If the quadrilateral is non-convex (has a \"dent\"), this interior angle will be greater than 180&deg;.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"340\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 165\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Convex Quad -->\n        <polygon points=\"30,130 55,35 135,45 145,130\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.1)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"90\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0369a1\">Convex (All angles &lt; 180°)</text>\n        <!-- Non-Convex (Dart) Quad -->\n        <polygon points=\"195,130 255,25 315,130 255,85\" fill=\"rgba(225,29,72,0.1)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <path d=\"M 241 95 A 18 18 0 1 1 269 95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" />\n        <text x=\"255\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">&gt; 180°</text>\n        <text x=\"255\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Non-Convex (Reflex interior angle)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Convex vs. Non-Convex quadrilateral showing internal reflex angle > 180°</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 2,
                            "q": "<p>In a quadrilateral ABCD, suppose AB || DC. Can ABCD be non-convex? What if we instead assume AB = CD? What if we instead assume &angle;A = &angle;C?</p>",
                            "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>If AB || DC:</strong> No, it cannot be non-convex. If two opposite sides are parallel, the consecutive interior angles must sum to 180&deg; (e.g., &angle;A + &angle;D = 180&deg;). Since their sum is 180&deg;, neither angle can be greater than 180&deg;. Thus, it must be convex.</li>\n    <li><strong>If AB = CD:</strong> Yes, it can be non-convex. A self-intersecting \"crossed\" quadrilateral or a non-convex dart shape can have a pair of opposite sides equal.</li>\n    <li><strong>If &angle;A = &angle;C:</strong> Yes, it can be non-convex. A dart shape (non-convex quadrilateral) can be symmetric such that its two non-reflex opposite angles are equal.</li>\n</ul>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 3,
                            "q": "<p>Consider three non-collinear points A, B, C and draw the lines AB, BC, CA. For every possible location of point D in the plane outside these lines, decide if ABCD is self-intersecting, non-convex, or convex.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The three lines extending from the triangle ABC divide the plane into 7 regions (the central triangle, and 6 outer regions). Depending on where point D is placed, the resulting sequence of vertices A-B-C-D forms different types of quadrilaterals.</p>\n<svg width=\"250\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">B</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">C</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">A</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"125\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"gray\">Convex</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"gray\">Self-Int.</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"110\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"gray\">Non-convex</text>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n    <li>If D is placed in the regions directly \"behind\" the vertices (e.g., opposite the triangle from A, B, or C), ABCD will be a <strong>self-intersecting</strong> (crossed) quadrilateral.</li>\n    <li>If D is placed in the regions bounded by the extended sides (the \"wedges\" pointing outwards from the edges), ABCD will be <strong>non-convex</strong> (dart-shaped).</li>\n    <li>If D is placed such that all internal angles remain less than 180&deg;, it forms a <strong>convex</strong> quadrilateral.</li>\n</ul>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 4,
                            "q": "<p><strong>1. True or false?</strong></p>\n<ol>\n    <li>(i) A parallelogram with a right angle is a rectangle.</li>\n    <li>(ii) A rhombus with perpendicular diagonals is a square.</li>\n    <li>(iii) If the diagonals of a parallelogram are equal, then it is a rectangle.</li>\n</ol>",
                            "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i) True.</strong> If one angle is 90&deg;, the opposite angle is also 90&deg;. The adjacent angles must sum to 180&deg;, making them 90&deg; as well. A quadrilateral with four right angles is a rectangle.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii) False.</strong> <em>Every</em> rhombus has perpendicular diagonals. It only becomes a square if its diagonals are also equal in length (or if it has a right angle).</li>\n    <li><strong>(iii) True.</strong> A parallelogram with equal diagonals guarantees that all four angles are 90&deg;, satisfying the definition of a rectangle.</li>\n</ul>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 5,
                            "q": "<p>The diagonal AC of a parallelogram ABCD bisects &angle;A. Show that it also bisects &angle;C and that ABCD is a rhombus.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. Since ABCD is a parallelogram, AB || DC and AD || BC.<br>\n2. Given AC bisects &angle;A, we have &angle;DAC = &angle;BAC.<br>\n3. Because AB || DC, the alternate interior angles are equal: &angle;BAC = &angle;DCA.<br>\n4. Similarly, because AD || BC, the alternate interior angles are equal: &angle;DAC = &angle;BCA.<br>\n5. By substitution, &angle;DCA = &angle;BCA. Thus, AC also bisects &angle;C.<br>\n6. In &Delta;ADC, since &angle;DAC = &angle;DCA, the sides opposite to equal angles are equal: AD = DC.<br>\n7. Since adjacent sides of the parallelogram are equal, all four sides must be equal (AB = DC = AD = BC). Therefore, ABCD is a rhombus.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"175\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 175\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Rhombus ABCD: A=(45,140), B=(165,140), C=(210,35), D=(90,35) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,140 165,140 210,35 90,35\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"30\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"172\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"216\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"73\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">D</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Diagonal AC bisects ∠A and ∠C ⇒ AD = DC ⇒ ABCD is a Rhombus</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 6,
                            "q": "<p>The following questions examine converses of true properties. Answer them with Yes or No. If your answer is No, what extra condition can you add so that the answer becomes Yes?</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>(i) If the diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD bisect its angles, must ABCD be a rhombus?</li>\n    <li>(ii) If the diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD bisect each other at right angles, must ABCD be a rhombus?</li>\n    <li>(iii) If the diagonals of a quadrilateral ABCD are of equal length, must ABCD be a rectangle?</li>\n</ul>",
                            "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i) Yes.</strong> If diagonals bisect the angles, you can prove through triangle congruence (ASA) that adjacent sides are equal, making it a rhombus.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii) Yes.</strong> If the diagonals bisect each other, it is guaranteed to be a parallelogram. If they do so at right angles, it guarantees all four sides are equal, making it a rhombus.</li>\n    <li><strong>(iii) No.</strong> An isosceles trapezium also has equal diagonals but is not a rectangle. <strong>Extra condition:</strong> Add \"If the diagonals <em>bisect each other</em> and are of equal length...\" then it becomes a rectangle.</li>\n</ul>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 7,
                            "q": "<p>Let ABCD be a parallelogram with AB &ne; BC. Show that the pairwise intersection points of the four angle bisectors form the vertices of a rectangle. Why did we assume AB &ne; BC?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\nLet the bisectors of &angle;A and &angle;B intersect at P, &angle;B and &angle;C intersect at Q, &angle;C and &angle;D intersect at R, and &angle;D and &angle;A intersect at S.<br>\n1. In a parallelogram, consecutive angles sum to 180&deg;. Therefore, &angle;A + &angle;D = 180&deg;.<br>\n2. The bisectors of A and D form &Delta;ASD. &angle;DAS + &angle;ADS = (&angle;A + &angle;D)/2 = 180&deg;/2 = 90&deg;.<br>\n3. The third angle of the triangle, &angle;ASD, must be 180&deg; - 90&deg; = 90&deg;.<br>\n4. By the same logic, the intersections at P, Q, and R also form 90&deg; angles. A quadrilateral PQRS with four right angles is a rectangle.<br>\n<strong>Why assume AB &ne; BC?</strong> If AB = BC, the parallelogram is a rhombus. In a rhombus, the angle bisectors are the diagonals themselves, meaning they intersect at exactly one point rather than forming a new four-sided region.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"290\" height=\"180\" viewBox=\"0 0 290 180\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Parallelogram ABCD: A=(35,145), B=(215,145), C=(255,35), D=(75,35) -->\n        <polygon points=\"35,145 215,145 255,35 75,35\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Inner rectangle PQRS formed by angle bisectors -->\n        <polygon points=\"125,112 162,75 165,68 128,105\" fill=\"none\" />\n        <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <line x1=\"75\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <line x1=\"255\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <line x1=\"215\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"116,93 145,67 174,87 145,113\" fill=\"rgba(225,29,72,0.18)\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"262\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"58\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"145\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#be123c\">PQRS (90°)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Pairwise intersections of the four angle bisectors of a parallelogram form rectangle PQRS</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 8,
                            "q": "<p>(i) If P, Q, R are the midpoints of sides AB, AC, BC respectively of &Delta;ABC, show that &Delta;PQR is congruent to &Delta;QPA and to two other triangles which you should identify.<br>\n(ii) Suppose someone erases &Delta;ABC, leaving only &Delta;PQR on the paper. Can you reconstruct &Delta;ABC from &Delta;PQR?</p>",
                            "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i)</strong> By the Midpoint Theorem, PQ || BC, QR || AB, and RP || AC. Because PQ || BR and PR || BQ, PBQR is a parallelogram. The diagonal PR divides it into two congruent triangles: &Delta;PQR &cong; &Delta;RBP. By the same logic for the other parallelograms, &Delta;PQR &cong; &Delta;QPA &cong; &Delta;CQR.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii) Yes, you can reconstruct &Delta;ABC.</strong> To find the original vertices A, B, and C, simply draw a line through point P parallel to QR, a line through Q parallel to PR, and a line through R parallel to PQ. The intersections of these three lines will perfectly recreate the original &Delta;ABC.</li>\n</ul>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"250\" height=\"190\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 190\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Triangle ABC: A=(125, 25), B=(35, 165), C=(215, 165) -->\n        <!-- Midpoints: P=(80, 95) on AB, Q=(170, 95) on AC, R=(125, 165) on BC -->\n        <polygon points=\"125,25 35,165 215,165\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"80,95 170,95 125,165\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"125\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"64\" y=\"96\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"176\" y=\"96\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">Q</text>\n        <text x=\"125\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">R</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Medial triangle ΔPQR divides ΔABC into 4 congruent triangles: ΔPQR ≅ ΔQPA ≅ ΔRBP ≅ ΔCQR</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 9,
                            "q": "<p>In &Delta;ABC, let M and N be midpoints of AB and AC respectively. Let D be any point on BC. Show that MN bisects AD.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. By the Midpoint Theorem, MN connects the midpoints of AB and AC, therefore MN || BC.<br>\n2. Let the segment AD intersect MN at point X.<br>\n3. In &Delta;ABD, M is the midpoint of AB, and the segment MX is part of the line MN, which is parallel to the base BD (since MN || BC).<br>\n4. By the Converse of the Midpoint Theorem (Theorem 7), a line drawn through the midpoint of one side of a triangle parallel to the base will bisect the third side.<br>\n5. Therefore, MX bisects AD, meaning X is the midpoint of AD. Thus, MN bisects AD.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"250\" height=\"190\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 190\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <polygon points=\"115,25 35,165 215,165\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Midsegment MN: M=(75, 95), N=(165, 95) -->\n        <line x1=\"75\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Segment AD: A=(115,25) to D=(145,165), midpoint X=(130, 95) -->\n        <line x1=\"115\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"145\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"95\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"115\" y=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"170\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"58\" y=\"98\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">M</text>\n        <text x=\"172\" y=\"98\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">N</text>\n        <text x=\"145\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"138\" y=\"88\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">X</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Midsegment MN ∥ BC bisects segment AD at X (AX = XD)</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 10,
                            "q": "<p>Suppose the midpoints of sides AB, BC, CD and DA of a quadrilateral ABCD are P, Q, R and S respectively.<br>\n(i) Show that PR and QS bisect each other.<br>\n(ii) Show that if AC = BD, then PR and QS are perpendicular. Is the converse true?</p>",
                            "a": "<ul>\n    <li><strong>(i)</strong> By Varignon's Theorem (Theorem 9), joining the midpoints of any quadrilateral forms a parallelogram. Thus, PQRS is a parallelogram. In any parallelogram, the diagonals bisect each other. Therefore, PR and QS bisect each other.</li>\n    <li><strong>(ii)</strong> If the diagonals of the original quadrilateral are equal (AC = BD), then the sides of the Varignon parallelogram PQRS, which are half the lengths of the diagonals (PQ = SR = AC/2 and PS = QR = BD/2), must be equal to each other. A parallelogram with equal adjacent sides is a rhombus. The diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular. Thus, PR &perp; QS. <strong>Yes, the converse is true</strong>: If PR &perp; QS, then PQRS is a rhombus, meaning its sides are equal, which forces AC = BD.</li>\n</ul>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 200\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Quad ABCD: A=(40,160), B=(210,170), C=(225,45), D=(70,30) -->\n        <!-- Midpoints: P=(125,165), Q=(217.5,107.5), R=(147.5,37.5), S=(55,95) -->\n        <polygon points=\"40,160 210,170 225,45 70,30\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"125,165 217.5,107.5 147.5,37.5 55,95\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Diagonals PR and QS of Varignon parallelogram -->\n        <line x1=\"125\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"147.5\" y2=\"37.5\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"217.5\" y1=\"107.5\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <text x=\"25\" y=\"168\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"216\" y=\"178\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"232\" y=\"45\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"55\" y=\"28\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"125\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"226\" y=\"112\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Q</text>\n        <text x=\"148\" y=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">R</text>\n        <text x=\"38\" y=\"98\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">S</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Varignon Parallelogram PQRS: Diagonals PR and QS bisect each other</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 11,
                            "q": "<p>Justify why the plane cannot be tiled with a regular pentagon.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Tiling the plane requires shapes to fit together around a vertex such that the sum of the angles meeting at that vertex equals exactly 360&deg;, leaving no gaps or overlaps.<br>\nThe interior angle of a regular pentagon is 108&deg;.<br>\nIf we try to fit regular pentagons around a vertex, three pentagons give 108&deg; &times; 3 = 324&deg;. This leaves a gap of 36&deg;. A fourth pentagon would make the sum 432&deg;, causing an overlap. Because 360 is not evenly divisible by 108, the plane cannot be tiled with regular pentagons.</p>\n<div style=\"margin: 12px 0; text-align: center;\"><svg width=\"280\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 200\" style=\"background:#f8fafc; border:1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius:8px; max-width:100%;\">\n  <!-- Central vertex V at (140, 105) -->\n  <!-- Pentagon 1 (top-left) -->\n  <polygon points=\"140,105 90,105 75,57 115,28 155,57\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Pentagon 2 (bottom-left) -->\n  <polygon points=\"140,105 90,105 75,153 115,182 155,153\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- Pentagon 3 (right, rotated by 108 deg from P1 edge (140,105)-(155,57)) -->\n  <polygon points=\"140,105 155,57 203,72 203,122 155,137\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" fill-opacity=\"0.7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- 36 degree gap wedge between (140,105)-(155,137) and (140,105)-(155,153) -->\n  <path d=\"M 140 105 L 175 180 M 140 105 L 195 162\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"105\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"112\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e40af\">108°</text>\n  <text x=\"112\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">108°</text>\n  <text x=\"162\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#15803d\">108°</text>\n  <text x=\"208\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#dc2626\">36° Gap!</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">3 × 108° = 324° &lt; 360° (Cannot Tile)</text>\n</svg><div style=\"font-size: 0.85em; color: #64748b; margin-top: 4px;\">Three Regular Pentagons at a Vertex Leave a 36° Uncovered Gap (4 × 108° = 432° > 360° Overlaps)</div></div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "12.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 12.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 12.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 12,
                            "q": "<p>Using a fact about parallelograms, show how to tile the plane using any given triangle.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n1. Take any given triangle and create an exact duplicate of it.<br>\n2. Rotate the duplicate by 180&deg; and align it along one of the sides of the original triangle.<br>\n3. The two triangles together form a parallelogram. (Because alternate interior angles are equal, making opposite sides parallel).<br>\n4. As discussed at the beginning of the chapter, the plane can always be tiled using copies of any fixed parallelogram by creating a grid of continuous parallel lines. Therefore, by tiling parallelograms, we successfully tile the plane using the given triangle.</p>\n<svg width=\"200\" height=\"100\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <polygon points=\"10,90 90,90 50,10\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <polygon points=\"90,90 130,10 50,10\" fill=\"lightgreen\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n</svg>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 13,
                            "q": "<p>You know that the sum of angles of a quadrilateral is 360&deg;, even for a non-convex quadrilateral. Now consider a self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD, where AB and CD intersect at point E. Show that &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D < 360&deg;.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. A self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD with intersecting sides AB and CD at point E forms two distinct triangles: &Delta;ADE and &Delta;BCE.<br>\n2. The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is 180&deg;. So, for &Delta;ADE: &angle;A + &angle;D + &angle;AED = 180&deg;.<br>\n3. For &Delta;BCE: &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;BEC = 180&deg;.<br>\n4. Adding both equations together: &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D + &angle;AED + &angle;BEC = 360&deg;.<br>\n5. Angles &angle;AED and &angle;BEC are vertically opposite angles at intersection E, and they have a positive measure greater than 0&deg;.<br>\n6. Therefore, &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D = 360&deg; - (&angle;AED + &angle;BEC). Since we are subtracting a positive value from 360&deg;, it proves that &angle;A + &angle;B + &angle;C + &angle;D < 360&deg;.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Crossed quad ABCD where AB and CD intersect at E(130, 95) -->\n        <!-- A=(45, 40), D=(45, 150), B=(215, 150), C=(215, 40) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,40 130,95 45,150\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <polygon points=\"215,150 130,95 215,40\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"95\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"28\" y=\"42\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"28\" y=\"155\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"155\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"222\" y=\"42\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"130\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">E</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD forming ΔADE and ΔBCE: ∠A + ∠B + ∠C + ∠D < 360°</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 14,
                            "q": "<p>In a parallelogram ABCD, two points P and Q are taken on diagonal BD such that DP = BQ. Show that APCQ is a parallelogram.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. Let the diagonals AC and BD of the original parallelogram ABCD intersect at point O.<br>\n2. We know that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. Therefore, OA = OC and OD = OB.<br>\n3. It is given that DP = BQ. <br>\n4. Let's find the distances from the center O to points P and Q: <br>\n   OP = OD - DP<br>\n   OQ = OB - BQ<br>\n5. Since OD = OB and DP = BQ, it must be true that OP = OQ.<br>\n6. In the inner quadrilateral APCQ, the diagonals are AC and PQ. We already know OA = OC, and we just proved OP = OQ.<br>\n7. Since the diagonals of APCQ bisect each other at O, by the converse of the diagonal property (Theorem 4), APCQ is a parallelogram.</p>\n<svg width=\"250\" height=\"150\" viewBox=\"0 0 250 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <polygon points=\"50,120 200,120 230,30 80,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <line x1=\"50\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"1\" />\n  <polygon points=\"50,120 120,90 230,30 160,60\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"blue\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,4\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n  <text x=\"35\" y=\"130\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">A</text>\n  <text x=\"205\" y=\"135\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">B</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">C</text>\n  <text x=\"65\" y=\"30\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">D</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">P</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"75\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">Q</text>\n</svg>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q8",
                            "globalIndex": 15,
                            "q": "<p>The diagonals AC and BD of a parallelogram ABCD intersect at O. A line through O meets AB and CD at points P and Q respectively. Show that O is the midpoint of PQ.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. Because ABCD is a parallelogram, AB || DC.<br>\n2. Consider the triangles &Delta;APO and &Delta;CQO formed by the line passing through O.<br>\n3. The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so OA = OC.<br>\n4. Because AB || DC, the alternate interior angles formed by transversal AC are equal: &angle;PAO = &angle;QCO.<br>\n5. The vertically opposite angles at O are equal: &angle;AOP = &angle;COQ.<br>\n6. By Angle-Side-Angle (ASA) congruence, &Delta;APO &cong; &Delta;CQO.<br>\n7. Therefore, by Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles (CPCTC), OP = OQ. Thus, O is the midpoint of PQ.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"270\" height=\"180\" viewBox=\"0 0 270 180\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Parallelogram ABCD: A=(40,145), B=(200,145), C=(230,35), D=(70,35); Centre O=(135,90) -->\n        <polygon points=\"40,145 200,145 230,35 70,35\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"70\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <!-- Line PQ through O(135,90): P=(115,145) on AB, Q=(155,35) on CD -->\n        <line x1=\"115\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n        <circle cx=\"135\" cy=\"90\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#1e293b\" />\n        <text x=\"25\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"206\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"236\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"55\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"115\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"155\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Q</text>\n        <text x=\"148\" y=\"95\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">O</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">ΔAPO ≅ ΔCQO (by ASA) ⇒ OP = OQ</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q12",
                            "globalIndex": 16,
                            "q": "<p>Suppose ABCD is a parallelogram and M, N are midpoints of AB and CD respectively. Show that segments DM and BN trisect segment AC.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Proof:</strong><br>\n1. ABCD is a parallelogram, so AB || DC and AB = DC.<br>\n2. M and N are midpoints, so AM = MB = 1/2 AB, and DN = NC = 1/2 DC.<br>\n3. Because AB = DC, it implies AM = NC and MB = DN. Also, MB || DN.<br>\n4. A quadrilateral with one pair of equal and parallel opposite sides is a parallelogram (Theorem 5). Thus, MBND is a parallelogram, which means DM || BN.<br>\n5. Let DM intersect AC at point P, and BN intersect AC at point Q.<br>\n6. In &Delta;ABQ, M is the midpoint of AB, and MP || BQ (since DM || BN). By the Converse of the Midpoint Theorem, P must be the midpoint of AQ. Therefore, AP = PQ.<br>\n7. In &Delta;CDP, N is the midpoint of CD, and NQ || DP (since BN || DM). By the Converse of the Midpoint Theorem, Q must be the midpoint of PC. Therefore, PQ = QC.<br>\n8. Combining these gives AP = PQ = QC. The points P and Q divide the diagonal AC into three equal parts, proving that DM and BN trisect AC.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"280\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 280 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Parallelogram ABCD: A=(35,145), B=(195,145), C=(245,35), D=(85,35) -->\n        <!-- Midpoints: M=(115,145) on AB, N=(165,35) on CD -->\n        <polygon points=\"35,145 195,145 245,35 85,35\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"245\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"145\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"195\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"35\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Trisection points P=(105, 108.3), Q=(175, 71.7) -->\n        <circle cx=\"105\" cy=\"108.3\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <circle cx=\"175\" cy=\"71.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A</text>\n        <text x=\"202\" y=\"150\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B</text>\n        <text x=\"252\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C</text>\n        <text x=\"70\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">D</text>\n        <text x=\"115\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">M</text>\n        <text x=\"165\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">N</text>\n        <text x=\"92\" y=\"102\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">P</text>\n        <text x=\"182\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Q</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Parallel segments DM and BN trisect diagonal AC at P and Q (AP = PQ = QC)</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 4
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q17",
                            "globalIndex": 17,
                            "q": "<p>Show that the sum of angles of a non-planar quadrilateral is always less than 360&deg;.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\nThink of a non-planar (skew) quadrilateral ABCD folded along the diagonal AC like a hinge.<br>\n1. If the figure were completely flat (planar), the sum of the angles would be exactly 360&deg;.<br>\n2. When you fold the quadrilateral along AC to make it non-planar, the points B and D are brought closer together.<br>\n3. In 3D space, the straight-line distance between B and D becomes shorter. Because the side lengths AB, BC, CD, DA remain fixed, reducing the distance BD forces the angles at A (&angle;BAD) and C (&angle;BCD) to become strictly smaller (by the Law of Cosines for the hinge).<br>\n4. The angles at B (&angle;ABC) and D (&angle;ADC) remain unchanged because the triangles &Delta;ABC and &Delta;ADC themselves do not warp.<br>\n5. Since two interior angles shrink while the other two stay the same, the total sum of the four interior angles must strictly be less than 360&deg;.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 5
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q21",
                            "globalIndex": 18,
                            "q": "<p>Sum of angles of a polygon. What is the sum of angles of a (planar non-self-intersecting) n-gon? Find a formula in terms of n and prove it.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n1. The sum of the interior angles of a planar n-gon is given by the formula: <strong>180&deg; &times; (n - 2)</strong>.<br>\n2. <strong>Proof:</strong> Choose any single vertex of the n-gon. Draw diagonals from this chosen vertex to all other non-adjacent vertices.<br>\n3. Since you cannot draw a diagonal to the vertex itself, nor to the two immediately adjacent vertices, you will draw (n - 3) diagonals.<br>\n4. These (n - 3) diagonals perfectly divide the interior of the n-gon into exactly <strong>(n - 2) distinct triangles</strong>.<br>\n5. The sum of the interior angles of one triangle is 180&deg;. Because these triangles completely cover the n-gon without overlapping, the sum of all interior angles of the n-gon is simply the sum of the interior angles of these (n - 2) triangles.<br>\n6. Therefore, Sum = (n - 2) &times; 180&deg;.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 6
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "name": "Chapter 13: Two Variables, One Line",
                    "exercises": [
                        {
                            "id": "13.1",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 0,
                                    "q": "<p>Write a linear equation in two variables in which $a = 3$, $b = 0$ and $c = -\\frac{1}{5}$.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The standard form is $ax + by + c = 0$.<br>\n          Substituting the given values: $3x + 0y - \\frac{1}{5} = 0$.<br>\n          This can also be written simply as $3x - \\frac{1}{5} = 0$, or multiplied by 5 to remove the fraction: $15x - 1 = 0$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 1,
                                    "q": "<p>Complete the following table after expressing the given linear equations in standard form.</p>\n          <table>\n              <tr><th>Linear equation in two variables</th><th>Standard Form</th><th>Coefficient of x</th><th>Coefficient of y</th><th>Constant term</th></tr>\n              <tr><td>$y - 15 = \\sqrt{2}x$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y - 2x = 0$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$5x = 3y$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$x = 8$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y = 1$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n          </table>",
                                    "a": "<table>\n              <tr><th>Linear equation</th><th>Standard Form ($ax+by+c=0$)</th><th>Coefficient of x ($a$)</th><th>Coefficient of y ($b$)</th><th>Constant term ($c$)</th></tr>\n              <tr><td>$y - 15 = \\sqrt{2}x$</td><td>$-\\sqrt{2}x + y - 15 = 0$ (or $\\sqrt{2}x - y + 15 = 0$)</td><td>$-\\sqrt{2}$ (or $\\sqrt{2}$)</td><td>$1$ (or $-1$)</td><td>$-15$ (or $15$)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y - 2x = 0$</td><td>$-2x + 3y + 0 = 0$</td><td>$-2$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$5x = 3y$</td><td>$5x - 3y + 0 = 0$</td><td>$5$</td><td>$-3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$x = 8$</td><td>$1x + 0y - 8 = 0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y = 1$</td><td>$0x + 3y - 1 = 0$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n          </table>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 2,
                                    "q": "<p><br>\n          (i) The cost of a notebook is twice the cost of a pen. Consider the cost of a notebook to be ₹t and that of a pen to be ₹p. Charlie wrote $t = 2p$ whereas Meera wrote $p = 2t$. Which of these two representations is correct?<br>\n          (ii) In a one-day International Cricket match... two Indian batsmen together scored 176 runs. Manisha expressed this situation as $x + y = 176$, where the number of runs scored by one batsman is x, and the number of runs scored by the other is y. Is this a correct representation?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i)</strong> Charlie's representation, <strong>$t = 2p$</strong>, is correct. Since the notebook ($t$) is twice as expensive as the pen ($p$), you must multiply the cost of the pen by 2 to equal the cost of the notebook.<br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> <strong>Yes</strong>, this is a correct representation. The sum of the runs scored by the first batsman ($x$) and the second batsman ($y$) equals the total runs they scored together (176).</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "13.2",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 3,
                                    "q": "<p>Verify if the ordered pair $(4, 3)$ is a solution of $5x - 6y = 2$. Explain your reasoning.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Substitute $x = 4$ and $y = 3$ into the equation $5x - 6y = 2$.<br>\n          LHS = $5(4) - 6(3) = 20 - 18 = 2$.<br>\n          RHS = 2.<br>\n          Since LHS = RHS, the ordered pair $(4, 3)$ <strong>is a solution</strong> of the equation.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 4,
                                    "q": "<p>Find any two solutions for each of the following equations:<br>\n          (i) $7x - 3y = 21$<br>\n          (ii) $2x + 3y = 5$</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i) $7x - 3y = 21$</strong><br>\n          Let $x = 0$: $7(0) - 3y = 21 \\implies -3y = 21 \\implies y = -7$. Solution 1: $(0, -7)$.<br>\n          Let $y = 0$: $7x - 3(0) = 21 \\implies 7x = 21 \\implies x = 3$. Solution 2: $(3, 0)$.<br><br>\n          <strong>(ii) $2x + 3y = 5$</strong><br>\n          Let $x = 1$: $2(1) + 3y = 5 \\implies 3y = 3 \\implies y = 1$. Solution 1: $(1, 1)$.<br>\n          Let $y = 3$: $2x + 3(3) = 5 \\implies 2x = -4 \\implies x = -2$. Solution 2: $(-2, 3)$.<br>\n          <em>(Other valid solutions exist).</em></p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 5,
                                    "q": "<p>In the equations given below, m and n are unknown constants:<br>\n          $2mx + 3y = 7$<br>\n          $4x + ny = -10$<br>\n          If $(2, 1)$ is the solution of both equations, find the values of m and n.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Substitute $x = 2$ and $y = 1$ into both equations.<br>\n          First equation: $2m(2) + 3(1) = 7 \\implies 4m + 3 = 7 \\implies 4m = 4 \\implies \\mathbf{m = 1}$.<br>\n          Second equation: $4(2) + n(1) = -10 \\implies 8 + n = -10 \\implies \\mathbf{n = -18}$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q5",
                                    "globalIndex": 6,
                                    "q": "<p>Consider the graph of the equation $3x - 7y = 21$ shown below. Does the point C $(2, 3)$ lie on the line? Does it satisfy the equation? Can points that do not lie on the line satisfy the equation?</p>\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 15px 0;\">\n            <svg width=\"250\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"-50 -50 200 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n              <line x1=\"-50\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"green\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n              <line x1=\"-50\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"black\"/> <!-- x-axis -->\n              <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-50\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\"/> <!-- y-axis -->\n              <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"black\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"90\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">A(7, 0)</text>\n              <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"black\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">B(0, -3)</text>\n              <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"70\" r=\"3\" fill=\"black\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"65\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">C(2, 3)</text>\n            </svg>\n          </div>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          - Look at the graph: Point C $(2, 3)$ is clearly floating above the green line, so it <strong>does not lie on the line</strong>.<br>\n          - Let's check algebraically: Substitute $x=2, y=3$ into $3x - 7y = 21$.<br>\n            LHS = $3(2) - 7(3) = 6 - 21 = -15$. Since $-15 \\neq 21$, it <strong>does not satisfy the equation</strong>.<br>\n          - <strong>No</strong>, points that do not lie on the line can never satisfy the equation, because the line is the exact graphical representation of all the solutions to that equation.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "13.3",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 7,
                                    "q": "<p>The following diagrams represent ski hills. Rank the hills in order of their steepness, from least to greatest.</p>\n          <ul>\n              <li><strong>Hill A:</strong> Rise = 60m, Run = 70m</li>\n              <li><strong>Hill B:</strong> Rise = 60m, Run = 110m</li>\n              <li><strong>Hill C:</strong> Rise = 80m, Run = 100m</li>\n          </ul>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"450\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 450 165\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Hill A: Rise=60, Run=70 -->\n        <polygon points=\"30,130 100,130 100,70\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"65\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Run = 70 m</text>\n        <text x=\"106\" y=\"104\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">60 m</text>\n        <text x=\"65\" y=\"45\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\">Hill A</text>\n        <!-- Hill B: Rise=60, Run=110 -->\n        <polygon points=\"150,130 260,130 260,70\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"205\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Run = 110 m</text>\n        <text x=\"266\" y=\"104\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\">60 m</text>\n        <text x=\"205\" y=\"45\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Hill B</text>\n        <!-- Hill C: Rise=80, Run=100 -->\n        <polygon points=\"310,130 410,130 410,50\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"360\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Run = 100 m</text>\n        <text x=\"415\" y=\"95\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#d97706\">80 m</text>\n        <text x=\"360\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">Hill C</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Ski Hills Comparison: Hill A (Slope ≈ 0.857), Hill B (Slope ≈ 0.545), Hill C (Slope = 0.800)</div>\n</div>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Steepness is determined by the slope (Rise / Run).<br>\n          Slope of A = $60 / 70 \\approx 0.857$<br>\n          Slope of B = $60 / 110 \\approx 0.545$<br>\n          Slope of C = $80 / 100 = 0.800$<br>\n          Ranking from least to greatest steepness: <strong>B, C, A</strong>.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 8,
                                    "q": "<p>The ramp at a loading dock rises 2.5 metres over a run of 4 metres. Find the slope of the ramp.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Slope = $\\frac{\\text{Rise}}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{2.5}{4} = 0.625$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 9,
                                    "q": "<p>An accessibility ramp for wheelchairs is to be made alongside the staircase. The guidelines given for the slope of the ramp is 1 cm vertical rise to 12 cm horizontal length. What should be the horizontal length of the ramp if the total height of the stairs is 18 cm?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The required slope is $\\frac{\\text{Rise}}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{1}{12}$.<br>\n          Given total rise = 18 cm.<br>\n          $\\frac{18}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{1}{12} \\implies \\text{Run} = 18 \\times 12 = 216$ cm.<br>\n          The horizontal length should be <strong>216 cm</strong> (or 2.16 metres).</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "13.4",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 10,
                                    "q": "<p>Given below are some everyday situations. Construct a pair of linear equations in two variables for each situation:<br>Let the cost of one movie ticket be ₹x and the cost of one snack box be ₹y.<br>\n          (i) On the first day, the family buys 2 movie tickets and 3 snack boxes for ₹850.<br>\n          (ii) On the second day, the family buys 4 movie tickets and 1 snack box for ₹1100.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          (i) $2x + 3y = 850$<br>\n          (ii) $4x + 1y = 1100$</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 11,
                                    "q": "<p>Let the fixed charge for one taxi trip be ₹x and the additional charge per kilometre be ₹y.<br>\n          (i) Sahil travelled 6 km and paid a total fare of ₹122.<br>\n          (ii) Meena travelled 8 km and paid a total fare of ₹160.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          (i) $x + 6y = 122$<br>\n          (ii) $x + 8y = 160$</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 12,
                                    "q": "<p>In a sports meet, tickets for adults cost ₹150 each and tickets for children cost ₹100 each. A total of 200 people attended the meet, and the total amount collected from ticket sales was ₹25,000. (Let the number of adult tickets sold be x and the number of children's tickets sold be y).</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Total people equation: $x + y = 200$<br>\n          Total amount equation: $150x + 100y = 25000$</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "13.5",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1(i)",
                                    "globalIndex": 13,
                                    "q": "<p>Form a pair of linear equations for each of the following problems and find their solutions.<br>\n          (i) The sum of two integers is +5 and their difference is -21. Find the two numbers.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Equations: $x + y = 5$ (1), $x - y = -21$ (2)<br>\n          Using elimination (add both): $2x = -16 \\implies x = -8$<br>\n          Substitute $x$ in (1): $-8 + y = 5 \\implies y = 13$<br>\n          The numbers are <strong>-8 and 13</strong>.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.5",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1(ii)",
                                    "globalIndex": 14,
                                    "q": "<p>(ii) The difference between two numbers is 26 and one number is three times the other. Find the numbers.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Equations: $x - y = 26$ (1), $x = 3y$ (2) (assuming $x > y$)<br>\n          Substitute (2) into (1): $3y - y = 26 \\implies 2y = 26 \\implies y = 13$<br>\n          Substitute $y$ in (2): $x = 3(13) = 39$<br>\n          The numbers are <strong>39 and 13</strong>.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.5",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1(iv)",
                                    "globalIndex": 15,
                                    "q": "<p>(iv) Taxi charges consist of a fixed charge plus a per km charge. For 10 km, paid ₹155. For 15 km, paid ₹220. Find fixed charge and charge per km. Cost for 25 km?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Let fixed charge = $x$, charge/km = $y$.<br>\n          Equations: $x + 10y = 155$ (1), $x + 15y = 220$ (2)<br>\n          Subtract (1) from (2): $5y = 65 \\implies y = 13$ (Charge per km is ₹13)<br>\n          Substitute $y$ in (1): $x + 10(13) = 155 \\implies x = 155 - 130 = 25$ (Fixed charge is ₹25)<br>\n          Cost for 25 km: $x + 25y = 25 + 25(13) = 25 + 325 = \\mathbf{350}$ rupees.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.5",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1(vii)",
                                    "globalIndex": 16,
                                    "q": "<p>(vii) Five years ago, Nuri was thrice as old as Sonu. Ten years later, Nuri will be twice as old as Sonu. How old are Nuri and Sonu?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Let Nuri's current age = $N$, Sonu's = $S$.<br>\n          5 years ago: $N - 5 = 3(S - 5) \\implies N - 3S = -10$ (1)<br>\n          10 years later: $N + 10 = 2(S + 10) \\implies N - 2S = 10$ (2)<br>\n          Subtract (1) from (2): $(N - 2S) - (N - 3S) = 10 - (-10) \\implies S = 20$<br>\n          Substitute $S=20$ in (2): $N - 2(20) = 10 \\implies N = 50$<br>\n          <strong>Nuri is 50, Sonu is 20.</strong></p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.5",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 17,
                                    "q": "<p>Use the ratios $\\frac{a_1}{a_2}, \\frac{b_1}{b_2}, \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$ to determine whether the lines representing the following pairs of linear equations intersect at a point, are parallel or are coincident.<br>\n          (i) $5x - 4y + 8 = 0$; $7x + 6y - 9 = 0$<br>\n          (ii) $9x + 3y + 12 = 0$; $18x + 6y + 24 = 0$<br>\n          (iii) $6x - 3y + 10 = 0$; $2x - y + 9 = 0$</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i)</strong> $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{5}{7}$, $\\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{-4}{6} = \\frac{-2}{3}$. Since $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} \\neq \\frac{b_1}{b_2}$, they <strong>intersect at a point</strong> (unique solution).<br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{9}{18} = \\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{c_1}{c_2} = \\frac{12}{24} = \\frac{1}{2}$. Since $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$, they are <strong>coincident</strong> (infinitely many solutions).<br>\n          <strong>(iii)</strong> $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$, $\\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{-3}{-1} = 3$, $\\frac{c_1}{c_2} = \\frac{10}{9}$. Since $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} \\neq \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$, they are <strong>parallel</strong> (no solution).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"430\" height=\"145\" viewBox=\"0 0 430 145\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- (i) Intersecting -->\n        <line x1=\"25\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"25\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"68\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#0f172a\" />\n        <text x=\"70\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(i) Intersecting (1 Sol)</text>\n        <!-- (ii) Coincident -->\n        <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" opacity=\"0.4\" />\n        <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\" />\n        <text x=\"215\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(ii) Coincident (∞ Sols)</text>\n        <!-- (iii) Parallel -->\n        <line x1=\"310\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"310\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"45\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"355\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(iii) Parallel (0 Sols)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Geometric interpretation of linear systems: Intersecting, Coincident, and Parallel lines</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.5",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 4
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q8",
                                    "globalIndex": 18,
                                    "q": "<p>5 pencils and 7 pens together cost ₹50, whereas 7 pencils and 5 pens together cost ₹46. Find the cost of each pencil and pen.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Let pencil cost = $x$, pen cost = $y$.<br>\n          $5x + 7y = 50$ (1)<br>\n          $7x + 5y = 46$ (2)<br>\n          Add (1) and (2): $12x + 12y = 96 \\implies x + y = 8$ (3)<br>\n          Subtract (2) from (1): $-2x + 2y = 4 \\implies -x + y = 2$ (4)<br>\n          Add (3) and (4): $2y = 10 \\implies y = 5$<br>\n          Substitute $y=5$ in (3): $x + 5 = 8 \\implies x = 3$<br>\n          <strong>Cost of pencil = ₹3, Cost of pen = ₹5.</strong></p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "13.5",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 5
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "end-of-chapter",
                            "name": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "title": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 19,
                                    "q": "<p>The graph of the line $y=3x$ passing through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, 6)$ is given below.<br>\n          (i) Identify the slope of the line from the graph and explain how you calculated it.<br>\n          (ii) What is the y-intercept of the line? Explain its significance.<br>\n          (iv) Without drawing a new graph, determine whether the point $(4, 10)$ lies on this line. Justify your answer.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"270\" height=\"240\" viewBox=\"0 0 270 240\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Grid lines: Origin at (60, 195); x scale: 35px/unit (0..4); y scale: 22px/unit (0..7) -->\n        <g stroke=\"#e2e8f0\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n          <line x1=\"95\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"215\" /><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"215\" />\n          <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"215\" /><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"215\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"173\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"173\" /><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"151\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"129\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"129\" /><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"107\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"107\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"85\" /><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"63\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"63\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"41\" />\n        </g>\n        <!-- Axes -->\n        <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"195\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Rise/Run triangle from (0,0)=(60,195) to (2,0)=(130,195) to (2,6)=(130,63) -->\n        <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"195\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"63\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <!-- Line y = 3x from (-0.3, -0.9)=(49.5, 214.8) to (2.4, 7.2)=(144, 36.6) -->\n        <line x1=\"49.5\" y1=\"214.8\" x2=\"144\" y2=\"36.6\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n        <!-- Points (0,0) and (2,6) -->\n        <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"195\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"63\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"48\" y=\"210\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(0, 0)</text>\n        <text x=\"138\" y=\"62\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">(2, 6)</text>\n        <text x=\"165\" y=\"132\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Rise = 6</text>\n        <text x=\"95\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Run = 2</text>\n        <text x=\"95\" y=\"95\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">y = 3x</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Graph of the line y = 3x passing through (0, 0) and (2, 6) with Slope = 6/2 = 3</div>\n</div>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i)</strong> Slope = $\\frac{\\text{Rise}}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{6 - 0}{2 - 0} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$.<br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> The line passes through $(0,0)$, so the y-intercept is 0. This signifies that at the starting point ($x=0$), the value of $y$ is also 0.<br>\n          <strong>(iv)</strong> No. Substitute $x=4$ into the equation $y=3x$. $y = 3(4) = 12$. Since $12 \\neq 10$, the point $(4, 10)$ does not lie on the line.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 20,
                                    "q": "<p>The formula to convert Celsius to Fahrenheit is $F = \\frac{9}{5}C + 32$.<br>\n          (ii) If the temperature is $30^\\circ C$, what is the temperature in Fahrenheit?<br>\n          (iii) If the temperature is $95^\\circ F$, what is the temperature in Celsius?<br>\n          (v) Is there a temperature which is numerically the same in both Fahrenheit and Celsius? If yes, find it.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> $F = \\frac{9}{5}(30) + 32 = 9(6) + 32 = 54 + 32 = \\mathbf{86^\\circ F}$.<br>\n          <strong>(iii)</strong> $95 = \\frac{9}{5}C + 32 \\implies 63 = \\frac{9}{5}C \\implies C = \\frac{63 \\times 5}{9} = 7 \\times 5 = \\mathbf{35^\\circ C}$.<br>\n          <strong>(v)</strong> Yes. Set $F = C = x$.<br>\n          $x = \\frac{9}{5}x + 32 \\implies x - \\frac{9}{5}x = 32 \\implies -\\frac{4}{5}x = 32 \\implies x = -\\frac{32 \\times 5}{4} = -4 \\times 5 = \\mathbf{-40}$. So, $-40^\\circ C = -40^\\circ F$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q5",
                                    "globalIndex": 21,
                                    "q": "<p>Give a formula to find the x-intercept of the line $y = mx + c$.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The x-intercept occurs where the line crosses the x-axis, meaning $y = 0$.<br>\n          Substitute $y=0$ into the equation: $0 = mx + c \\implies mx = -c \\implies x = -\\frac{c}{m}$.<br>\n          The formula for the x-intercept is <strong>$-\\frac{c}{m}$</strong> (assuming $m \\neq 0$).</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q6",
                                    "globalIndex": 22,
                                    "q": "<p>Plan A: ₹50 monthly fee + ₹0.20 per minute. Plan B: ₹30 monthly fee + ₹0.30 per minute.<br>\n          Find the number of minutes at which both plans cost the same.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the number of minutes be $x$.<br>\n          Cost of Plan A = $50 + 0.20x$<br>\n          Cost of Plan B = $30 + 0.30x$<br>\n          Equate them: $50 + 0.20x = 30 + 0.30x \\implies 20 = 0.10x \\implies x = \\frac{20}{0.10} = 200$.<br>\n          Both plans cost the same at <strong>200 minutes</strong>. (Below 200 mins, Plan B is cheaper; above 200 mins, Plan A is cheaper).</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q8",
                                    "globalIndex": 23,
                                    "q": "<p>For what values of $p$ does the pair of equations $4x + py + 8 = 0$ and $2x + 2y + 2 = 0$ have a unique solution?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> For a unique solution, the ratio of coefficients must be $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} \\neq \\frac{b_1}{b_2}$.<br>\n          $\\frac{4}{2} \\neq \\frac{p}{2} \\implies 2 \\neq \\frac{p}{2} \\implies p \\neq 4$.<br>\n          The equations have a unique solution for <strong>all real values of $p$ except $p = 4$</strong>.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 4
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q10",
                                    "globalIndex": 24,
                                    "q": "<p>Find the value of 'k' for which the following system of equations represents a pair of coincident lines:<br>\n          $x + 2y = 3$; $(k-1)x + (k+1)y = k+3$.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> For coincident lines (infinitely many solutions), $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$.<br>\n          $\\frac{1}{k-1} = \\frac{2}{k+1} = \\frac{3}{k+3}$<br>\n          Solve the first part: $\\frac{1}{k-1} = \\frac{2}{k+1} \\implies k+1 = 2(k-1) \\implies k+1 = 2k-2 \\implies 3 = k$.<br>\n          Verify with the third part: $\\frac{2}{3+1} = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{3}{3+3} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$. It matches.<br>\n          So, <strong>k = 3</strong>.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 5
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ],
                    "solutions": [
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 0,
                            "q": "<p>Write a linear equation in two variables in which $a = 3$, $b = 0$ and $c = -\\frac{1}{5}$.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The standard form is $ax + by + c = 0$.<br>\n          Substituting the given values: $3x + 0y - \\frac{1}{5} = 0$.<br>\n          This can also be written simply as $3x - \\frac{1}{5} = 0$, or multiplied by 5 to remove the fraction: $15x - 1 = 0$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 1,
                            "q": "<p>Complete the following table after expressing the given linear equations in standard form.</p>\n          <table>\n              <tr><th>Linear equation in two variables</th><th>Standard Form</th><th>Coefficient of x</th><th>Coefficient of y</th><th>Constant term</th></tr>\n              <tr><td>$y - 15 = \\sqrt{2}x$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y - 2x = 0$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$5x = 3y$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$x = 8$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y = 1$</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n          </table>",
                            "a": "<table>\n              <tr><th>Linear equation</th><th>Standard Form ($ax+by+c=0$)</th><th>Coefficient of x ($a$)</th><th>Coefficient of y ($b$)</th><th>Constant term ($c$)</th></tr>\n              <tr><td>$y - 15 = \\sqrt{2}x$</td><td>$-\\sqrt{2}x + y - 15 = 0$ (or $\\sqrt{2}x - y + 15 = 0$)</td><td>$-\\sqrt{2}$ (or $\\sqrt{2}$)</td><td>$1$ (or $-1$)</td><td>$-15$ (or $15$)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y - 2x = 0$</td><td>$-2x + 3y + 0 = 0$</td><td>$-2$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$5x = 3y$</td><td>$5x - 3y + 0 = 0$</td><td>$5$</td><td>$-3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$x = 8$</td><td>$1x + 0y - 8 = 0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>$3y = 1$</td><td>$0x + 3y - 1 = 0$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n          </table>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 2,
                            "q": "<p><br>\n          (i) The cost of a notebook is twice the cost of a pen. Consider the cost of a notebook to be ₹t and that of a pen to be ₹p. Charlie wrote $t = 2p$ whereas Meera wrote $p = 2t$. Which of these two representations is correct?<br>\n          (ii) In a one-day International Cricket match... two Indian batsmen together scored 176 runs. Manisha expressed this situation as $x + y = 176$, where the number of runs scored by one batsman is x, and the number of runs scored by the other is y. Is this a correct representation?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i)</strong> Charlie's representation, <strong>$t = 2p$</strong>, is correct. Since the notebook ($t$) is twice as expensive as the pen ($p$), you must multiply the cost of the pen by 2 to equal the cost of the notebook.<br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> <strong>Yes</strong>, this is a correct representation. The sum of the runs scored by the first batsman ($x$) and the second batsman ($y$) equals the total runs they scored together (176).</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 3,
                            "q": "<p>Verify if the ordered pair $(4, 3)$ is a solution of $5x - 6y = 2$. Explain your reasoning.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Substitute $x = 4$ and $y = 3$ into the equation $5x - 6y = 2$.<br>\n          LHS = $5(4) - 6(3) = 20 - 18 = 2$.<br>\n          RHS = 2.<br>\n          Since LHS = RHS, the ordered pair $(4, 3)$ <strong>is a solution</strong> of the equation.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 4,
                            "q": "<p>Find any two solutions for each of the following equations:<br>\n          (i) $7x - 3y = 21$<br>\n          (ii) $2x + 3y = 5$</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i) $7x - 3y = 21$</strong><br>\n          Let $x = 0$: $7(0) - 3y = 21 \\implies -3y = 21 \\implies y = -7$. Solution 1: $(0, -7)$.<br>\n          Let $y = 0$: $7x - 3(0) = 21 \\implies 7x = 21 \\implies x = 3$. Solution 2: $(3, 0)$.<br><br>\n          <strong>(ii) $2x + 3y = 5$</strong><br>\n          Let $x = 1$: $2(1) + 3y = 5 \\implies 3y = 3 \\implies y = 1$. Solution 1: $(1, 1)$.<br>\n          Let $y = 3$: $2x + 3(3) = 5 \\implies 2x = -4 \\implies x = -2$. Solution 2: $(-2, 3)$.<br>\n          <em>(Other valid solutions exist).</em></p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 5,
                            "q": "<p>In the equations given below, m and n are unknown constants:<br>\n          $2mx + 3y = 7$<br>\n          $4x + ny = -10$<br>\n          If $(2, 1)$ is the solution of both equations, find the values of m and n.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Substitute $x = 2$ and $y = 1$ into both equations.<br>\n          First equation: $2m(2) + 3(1) = 7 \\implies 4m + 3 = 7 \\implies 4m = 4 \\implies \\mathbf{m = 1}$.<br>\n          Second equation: $4(2) + n(1) = -10 \\implies 8 + n = -10 \\implies \\mathbf{n = -18}$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q5",
                            "globalIndex": 6,
                            "q": "<p>Consider the graph of the equation $3x - 7y = 21$ shown below. Does the point C $(2, 3)$ lie on the line? Does it satisfy the equation? Can points that do not lie on the line satisfy the equation?</p>\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 15px 0;\">\n            <svg width=\"250\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"-50 -50 200 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n              <line x1=\"-50\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"green\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n              <line x1=\"-50\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"black\"/> <!-- x-axis -->\n              <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-50\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\"/> <!-- y-axis -->\n              <circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"100\" r=\"3\" fill=\"black\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"90\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">A(7, 0)</text>\n              <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"black\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"145\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">B(0, -3)</text>\n              <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"70\" r=\"3\" fill=\"black\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"65\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"12\">C(2, 3)</text>\n            </svg>\n          </div>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          - Look at the graph: Point C $(2, 3)$ is clearly floating above the green line, so it <strong>does not lie on the line</strong>.<br>\n          - Let's check algebraically: Substitute $x=2, y=3$ into $3x - 7y = 21$.<br>\n            LHS = $3(2) - 7(3) = 6 - 21 = -15$. Since $-15 \\neq 21$, it <strong>does not satisfy the equation</strong>.<br>\n          - <strong>No</strong>, points that do not lie on the line can never satisfy the equation, because the line is the exact graphical representation of all the solutions to that equation.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 7,
                            "q": "<p>The following diagrams represent ski hills. Rank the hills in order of their steepness, from least to greatest.</p>\n          <ul>\n              <li><strong>Hill A:</strong> Rise = 60m, Run = 70m</li>\n              <li><strong>Hill B:</strong> Rise = 60m, Run = 110m</li>\n              <li><strong>Hill C:</strong> Rise = 80m, Run = 100m</li>\n          </ul>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"450\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 450 165\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Hill A: Rise=60, Run=70 -->\n        <polygon points=\"30,130 100,130 100,70\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"65\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Run = 70 m</text>\n        <text x=\"106\" y=\"104\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">60 m</text>\n        <text x=\"65\" y=\"45\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\">Hill A</text>\n        <!-- Hill B: Rise=60, Run=110 -->\n        <polygon points=\"150,130 260,130 260,70\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.15)\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"205\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Run = 110 m</text>\n        <text x=\"266\" y=\"104\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\">60 m</text>\n        <text x=\"205\" y=\"45\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Hill B</text>\n        <!-- Hill C: Rise=80, Run=100 -->\n        <polygon points=\"310,130 410,130 410,50\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.15)\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"360\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Run = 100 m</text>\n        <text x=\"415\" y=\"95\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#d97706\">80 m</text>\n        <text x=\"360\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">Hill C</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Ski Hills Comparison: Hill A (Slope ≈ 0.857), Hill B (Slope ≈ 0.545), Hill C (Slope = 0.800)</div>\n</div>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Steepness is determined by the slope (Rise / Run).<br>\n          Slope of A = $60 / 70 \\approx 0.857$<br>\n          Slope of B = $60 / 110 \\approx 0.545$<br>\n          Slope of C = $80 / 100 = 0.800$<br>\n          Ranking from least to greatest steepness: <strong>B, C, A</strong>.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 8,
                            "q": "<p>The ramp at a loading dock rises 2.5 metres over a run of 4 metres. Find the slope of the ramp.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Slope = $\\frac{\\text{Rise}}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{2.5}{4} = 0.625$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 9,
                            "q": "<p>An accessibility ramp for wheelchairs is to be made alongside the staircase. The guidelines given for the slope of the ramp is 1 cm vertical rise to 12 cm horizontal length. What should be the horizontal length of the ramp if the total height of the stairs is 18 cm?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The required slope is $\\frac{\\text{Rise}}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{1}{12}$.<br>\n          Given total rise = 18 cm.<br>\n          $\\frac{18}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{1}{12} \\implies \\text{Run} = 18 \\times 12 = 216$ cm.<br>\n          The horizontal length should be <strong>216 cm</strong> (or 2.16 metres).</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 10,
                            "q": "<p>Given below are some everyday situations. Construct a pair of linear equations in two variables for each situation:<br>Let the cost of one movie ticket be ₹x and the cost of one snack box be ₹y.<br>\n          (i) On the first day, the family buys 2 movie tickets and 3 snack boxes for ₹850.<br>\n          (ii) On the second day, the family buys 4 movie tickets and 1 snack box for ₹1100.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          (i) $2x + 3y = 850$<br>\n          (ii) $4x + 1y = 1100$</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 11,
                            "q": "<p>Let the fixed charge for one taxi trip be ₹x and the additional charge per kilometre be ₹y.<br>\n          (i) Sahil travelled 6 km and paid a total fare of ₹122.<br>\n          (ii) Meena travelled 8 km and paid a total fare of ₹160.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          (i) $x + 6y = 122$<br>\n          (ii) $x + 8y = 160$</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 12,
                            "q": "<p>In a sports meet, tickets for adults cost ₹150 each and tickets for children cost ₹100 each. A total of 200 people attended the meet, and the total amount collected from ticket sales was ₹25,000. (Let the number of adult tickets sold be x and the number of children's tickets sold be y).</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Total people equation: $x + y = 200$<br>\n          Total amount equation: $150x + 100y = 25000$</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1(i)",
                            "globalIndex": 13,
                            "q": "<p>Form a pair of linear equations for each of the following problems and find their solutions.<br>\n          (i) The sum of two integers is +5 and their difference is -21. Find the two numbers.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Equations: $x + y = 5$ (1), $x - y = -21$ (2)<br>\n          Using elimination (add both): $2x = -16 \\implies x = -8$<br>\n          Substitute $x$ in (1): $-8 + y = 5 \\implies y = 13$<br>\n          The numbers are <strong>-8 and 13</strong>.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.5",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1(ii)",
                            "globalIndex": 14,
                            "q": "<p>(ii) The difference between two numbers is 26 and one number is three times the other. Find the numbers.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Equations: $x - y = 26$ (1), $x = 3y$ (2) (assuming $x > y$)<br>\n          Substitute (2) into (1): $3y - y = 26 \\implies 2y = 26 \\implies y = 13$<br>\n          Substitute $y$ in (2): $x = 3(13) = 39$<br>\n          The numbers are <strong>39 and 13</strong>.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.5",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1(iv)",
                            "globalIndex": 15,
                            "q": "<p>(iv) Taxi charges consist of a fixed charge plus a per km charge. For 10 km, paid ₹155. For 15 km, paid ₹220. Find fixed charge and charge per km. Cost for 25 km?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Let fixed charge = $x$, charge/km = $y$.<br>\n          Equations: $x + 10y = 155$ (1), $x + 15y = 220$ (2)<br>\n          Subtract (1) from (2): $5y = 65 \\implies y = 13$ (Charge per km is ₹13)<br>\n          Substitute $y$ in (1): $x + 10(13) = 155 \\implies x = 155 - 130 = 25$ (Fixed charge is ₹25)<br>\n          Cost for 25 km: $x + 25y = 25 + 25(13) = 25 + 325 = \\mathbf{350}$ rupees.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.5",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1(vii)",
                            "globalIndex": 16,
                            "q": "<p>(vii) Five years ago, Nuri was thrice as old as Sonu. Ten years later, Nuri will be twice as old as Sonu. How old are Nuri and Sonu?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Let Nuri's current age = $N$, Sonu's = $S$.<br>\n          5 years ago: $N - 5 = 3(S - 5) \\implies N - 3S = -10$ (1)<br>\n          10 years later: $N + 10 = 2(S + 10) \\implies N - 2S = 10$ (2)<br>\n          Subtract (1) from (2): $(N - 2S) - (N - 3S) = 10 - (-10) \\implies S = 20$<br>\n          Substitute $S=20$ in (2): $N - 2(20) = 10 \\implies N = 50$<br>\n          <strong>Nuri is 50, Sonu is 20.</strong></p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.5",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 17,
                            "q": "<p>Use the ratios $\\frac{a_1}{a_2}, \\frac{b_1}{b_2}, \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$ to determine whether the lines representing the following pairs of linear equations intersect at a point, are parallel or are coincident.<br>\n          (i) $5x - 4y + 8 = 0$; $7x + 6y - 9 = 0$<br>\n          (ii) $9x + 3y + 12 = 0$; $18x + 6y + 24 = 0$<br>\n          (iii) $6x - 3y + 10 = 0$; $2x - y + 9 = 0$</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i)</strong> $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{5}{7}$, $\\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{-4}{6} = \\frac{-2}{3}$. Since $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} \\neq \\frac{b_1}{b_2}$, they <strong>intersect at a point</strong> (unique solution).<br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{9}{18} = \\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$, $\\frac{c_1}{c_2} = \\frac{12}{24} = \\frac{1}{2}$. Since $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$, they are <strong>coincident</strong> (infinitely many solutions).<br>\n          <strong>(iii)</strong> $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$, $\\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{-3}{-1} = 3$, $\\frac{c_1}{c_2} = \\frac{10}{9}$. Since $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} \\neq \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$, they are <strong>parallel</strong> (no solution).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"430\" height=\"145\" viewBox=\"0 0 430 145\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- (i) Intersecting -->\n        <line x1=\"25\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"25\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"68\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#0f172a\" />\n        <text x=\"70\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(i) Intersecting (1 Sol)</text>\n        <!-- (ii) Coincident -->\n        <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" opacity=\"0.4\" />\n        <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\" />\n        <text x=\"215\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(ii) Coincident (∞ Sols)</text>\n        <!-- (iii) Parallel -->\n        <line x1=\"310\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <line x1=\"310\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"45\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <text x=\"355\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(iii) Parallel (0 Sols)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Geometric interpretation of linear systems: Intersecting, Coincident, and Parallel lines</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.5",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 4
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q8",
                            "globalIndex": 18,
                            "q": "<p>5 pencils and 7 pens together cost ₹50, whereas 7 pencils and 5 pens together cost ₹46. Find the cost of each pencil and pen.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          Let pencil cost = $x$, pen cost = $y$.<br>\n          $5x + 7y = 50$ (1)<br>\n          $7x + 5y = 46$ (2)<br>\n          Add (1) and (2): $12x + 12y = 96 \\implies x + y = 8$ (3)<br>\n          Subtract (2) from (1): $-2x + 2y = 4 \\implies -x + y = 2$ (4)<br>\n          Add (3) and (4): $2y = 10 \\implies y = 5$<br>\n          Substitute $y=5$ in (3): $x + 5 = 8 \\implies x = 3$<br>\n          <strong>Cost of pencil = ₹3, Cost of pen = ₹5.</strong></p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "13.5",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 13.5",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 5
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 19,
                            "q": "<p>The graph of the line $y=3x$ passing through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, 6)$ is given below.<br>\n          (i) Identify the slope of the line from the graph and explain how you calculated it.<br>\n          (ii) What is the y-intercept of the line? Explain its significance.<br>\n          (iv) Without drawing a new graph, determine whether the point $(4, 10)$ lies on this line. Justify your answer.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"270\" height=\"240\" viewBox=\"0 0 270 240\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Grid lines: Origin at (60, 195); x scale: 35px/unit (0..4); y scale: 22px/unit (0..7) -->\n        <g stroke=\"#e2e8f0\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n          <line x1=\"95\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"215\" /><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"215\" />\n          <line x1=\"165\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"215\" /><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"215\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"173\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"173\" /><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"151\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"129\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"129\" /><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"107\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"107\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"85\" /><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"63\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"63\" />\n          <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"41\" />\n        </g>\n        <!-- Axes -->\n        <line x1=\"35\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"195\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Rise/Run triangle from (0,0)=(60,195) to (2,0)=(130,195) to (2,6)=(130,63) -->\n        <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"195\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"195\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"63\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <!-- Line y = 3x from (-0.3, -0.9)=(49.5, 214.8) to (2.4, 7.2)=(144, 36.6) -->\n        <line x1=\"49.5\" y1=\"214.8\" x2=\"144\" y2=\"36.6\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n        <!-- Points (0,0) and (2,6) -->\n        <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"195\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"63\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"48\" y=\"210\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">(0, 0)</text>\n        <text x=\"138\" y=\"62\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">(2, 6)</text>\n        <text x=\"165\" y=\"132\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Rise = 6</text>\n        <text x=\"95\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Run = 2</text>\n        <text x=\"95\" y=\"95\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">y = 3x</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Graph of the line y = 3x passing through (0, 0) and (2, 6) with Slope = 6/2 = 3</div>\n</div>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(i)</strong> Slope = $\\frac{\\text{Rise}}{\\text{Run}} = \\frac{6 - 0}{2 - 0} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$.<br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> The line passes through $(0,0)$, so the y-intercept is 0. This signifies that at the starting point ($x=0$), the value of $y$ is also 0.<br>\n          <strong>(iv)</strong> No. Substitute $x=4$ into the equation $y=3x$. $y = 3(4) = 12$. Since $12 \\neq 10$, the point $(4, 10)$ does not lie on the line.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 20,
                            "q": "<p>The formula to convert Celsius to Fahrenheit is $F = \\frac{9}{5}C + 32$.<br>\n          (ii) If the temperature is $30^\\circ C$, what is the temperature in Fahrenheit?<br>\n          (iii) If the temperature is $95^\\circ F$, what is the temperature in Celsius?<br>\n          (v) Is there a temperature which is numerically the same in both Fahrenheit and Celsius? If yes, find it.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n          <strong>(ii)</strong> $F = \\frac{9}{5}(30) + 32 = 9(6) + 32 = 54 + 32 = \\mathbf{86^\\circ F}$.<br>\n          <strong>(iii)</strong> $95 = \\frac{9}{5}C + 32 \\implies 63 = \\frac{9}{5}C \\implies C = \\frac{63 \\times 5}{9} = 7 \\times 5 = \\mathbf{35^\\circ C}$.<br>\n          <strong>(v)</strong> Yes. Set $F = C = x$.<br>\n          $x = \\frac{9}{5}x + 32 \\implies x - \\frac{9}{5}x = 32 \\implies -\\frac{4}{5}x = 32 \\implies x = -\\frac{32 \\times 5}{4} = -4 \\times 5 = \\mathbf{-40}$. So, $-40^\\circ C = -40^\\circ F$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q5",
                            "globalIndex": 21,
                            "q": "<p>Give a formula to find the x-intercept of the line $y = mx + c$.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The x-intercept occurs where the line crosses the x-axis, meaning $y = 0$.<br>\n          Substitute $y=0$ into the equation: $0 = mx + c \\implies mx = -c \\implies x = -\\frac{c}{m}$.<br>\n          The formula for the x-intercept is <strong>$-\\frac{c}{m}$</strong> (assuming $m \\neq 0$).</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q6",
                            "globalIndex": 22,
                            "q": "<p>Plan A: ₹50 monthly fee + ₹0.20 per minute. Plan B: ₹30 monthly fee + ₹0.30 per minute.<br>\n          Find the number of minutes at which both plans cost the same.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the number of minutes be $x$.<br>\n          Cost of Plan A = $50 + 0.20x$<br>\n          Cost of Plan B = $30 + 0.30x$<br>\n          Equate them: $50 + 0.20x = 30 + 0.30x \\implies 20 = 0.10x \\implies x = \\frac{20}{0.10} = 200$.<br>\n          Both plans cost the same at <strong>200 minutes</strong>. (Below 200 mins, Plan B is cheaper; above 200 mins, Plan A is cheaper).</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q8",
                            "globalIndex": 23,
                            "q": "<p>For what values of $p$ does the pair of equations $4x + py + 8 = 0$ and $2x + 2y + 2 = 0$ have a unique solution?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> For a unique solution, the ratio of coefficients must be $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} \\neq \\frac{b_1}{b_2}$.<br>\n          $\\frac{4}{2} \\neq \\frac{p}{2} \\implies 2 \\neq \\frac{p}{2} \\implies p \\neq 4$.<br>\n          The equations have a unique solution for <strong>all real values of $p$ except $p = 4$</strong>.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 4
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q10",
                            "globalIndex": 24,
                            "q": "<p>Find the value of 'k' for which the following system of equations represents a pair of coincident lines:<br>\n          $x + 2y = 3$; $(k-1)x + (k+1)y = k+3$.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> For coincident lines (infinitely many solutions), $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$.<br>\n          $\\frac{1}{k-1} = \\frac{2}{k+1} = \\frac{3}{k+3}$<br>\n          Solve the first part: $\\frac{1}{k-1} = \\frac{2}{k+1} \\implies k+1 = 2(k-1) \\implies k+1 = 2k-2 \\implies 3 = k$.<br>\n          Verify with the third part: $\\frac{2}{3+1} = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{3}{3+3} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$. It matches.<br>\n          So, <strong>k = 3</strong>.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 5
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "name": "Chapter 14: Math of Space: Surface Area and Volume",
                    "exercises": [
                        {
                            "id": "14.1",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 0,
                                    "q": "<p>The volume of a cube is 64 cm³. What is its total surface area?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the side of the cube be $a$.<br>\nVolume = $a^3 = 64 \\Rightarrow a = 4$ cm.<br>\nThe Total Surface Area (TSA) of a cube is $6a^2$.<br>\nTSA = $6(4^2) = 6(16) = 96$ cm².</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 1,
                                    "q": "<p>How many small cubes with side 20 cm can be packed tight in a cubical box with side 2 m?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> First, convert the side of the large box to cm: $2$ m = $200$ cm.<br>\nVolume of the large box = $(200)^3 = 8,000,000$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of one small cube = $(20)^3 = 8,000$ cm³.<br>\nNumber of small cubes = $\\frac{8,000,000}{8,000} = 1000$ cubes.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 2,
                                    "q": "<p>The dimensions of a godown are $40 \\text{ m} \\times 25 \\text{ m} \\times 10 \\text{ m}$. If it is filled with cuboidal boxes, each of dimensions $2 \\text{ m} \\times 1.25 \\text{ m} \\times 1 \\text{ m}$, then find the number of boxes.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume of the godown = $40 \\times 25 \\times 10 = 10,000$ m³.<br>\nVolume of one box = $2 \\times 1.25 \\times 1 = 2.5$ m³.<br>\nNumber of boxes = $\\frac{10,000}{2.5} = 4000$ boxes.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 3,
                                    "q": "<p>Two cubes each of volume 125 cm³ are joined end to end. Find the surface area of the resulting cuboid.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume of one cube = $a^3 = 125 \\Rightarrow a = 5$ cm.<br>\nWhen joined end to end, the resulting cuboid has:<br>\nLength ($l$) = $5 + 5 = 10$ cm<br>\nWidth ($w$) = $5$ cm<br>\nHeight ($h$) = $5$ cm<br>\nTotal Surface Area = $2(lw + wh + hl) = 2((10)(5) + (5)(5) + (5)(10)) = 2(50 + 25 + 50) = 2(125) = 250$ cm².</p>\n<svg width=\"200\" height=\"100\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#e0e0e0\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#c0c0c0\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\"/>\n<text x=\"55\" y=\"55\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">5</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"55\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">5</text>\n</svg>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q5",
                                    "globalIndex": 4,
                                    "q": "<p>A cube of side 4 cm is cut into cubes of side 1 cm. What is the ratio of the surface areas of the original cube and all the cut-out cubes?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Surface area of the original cube = $6(4^2) = 6(16) = 96$ cm².<br>\nVolume of the original cube = $4^3 = 64$ cm³, which yields 64 smaller cubes of 1 cm³ each.<br>\nSurface area of one 1 cm cube = $6(1^2) = 6$ cm².<br>\nTotal surface area of all 64 smaller cubes = $64 \\times 6 = 384$ cm².<br>\nRatio = $96 : 384 = 1 : 4$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 4
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q6",
                                    "globalIndex": 5,
                                    "q": "<p>The surface areas of the three faces of a cuboid that meet at one of the corners of the cuboid are 6 cm², 15 cm², and 10 cm² respectively. What is the volume of the cuboid?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the dimensions be $l, w, h$.<br>\nGiven: $lw = 6$, $wh = 15$, $lh = 10$.<br>\nMultiply all three equations: $(lw)(wh)(lh) = 6 \\times 15 \\times 10$<br>\n$(lwh)^2 = 900$<br>\nVolume $V = lwh = \\sqrt{900} = 30$ cm³.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Front face (lh = 10 cm^2) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,70 165,70 165,150 45,150\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Top face (lw = 6 cm^2) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,70 90,35 210,35 165,70\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.18)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Right face (wh = 15 cm^2) -->\n        <polygon points=\"165,70 210,35 210,115 165,150\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.18)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"105\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\">lh = 10 cm²</text>\n        <text x=\"128\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">lw = 6 cm²</text>\n        <text x=\"188\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">wh=15</text>\n        <text x=\"105\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">V = √(6 × 15 × 10) = 30 cm³</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Cuboid with three concurrent face areas lw = 6 cm², wh = 15 cm², lh = 10 cm²</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 5
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q7",
                                    "globalIndex": 6,
                                    "q": "<p>A cube of side 5 cm is painted on all its faces. If it is sliced into 1 cm³ cubes, how many of these 1 cm³ cubes have:<br>\n(i) exactly three faces painted?<br>\n(ii) exactly two faces painted?<br>\n(iii) exactly one face painted?<br>\n(iv) no face painted?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Total smaller cubes = $5 \\times 5 \\times 5 = 125$.<br>\n(i) Exactly three faces painted (corners): <strong>8</strong> cubes.<br>\n(ii) Exactly two faces painted (edges, excluding corners): 12 edges $\\times$ (5 - 2) = $12 \\times 3 = $ <strong>36</strong> cubes.<br>\n(iii) Exactly one face painted (centers of faces): 6 faces $\\times$ (3 $\\times$ 3) = $6 \\times 9 = $ <strong>54</strong> cubes.<br>\n(iv) No face painted (interior cubes): $(5 - 2) \\times (5 - 2) \\times (5 - 2) = 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = $ <strong>27</strong> cubes.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"240\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 200\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- 5x5 front face grid at (55, 30), cell=26px -> 130x130 -->\n        <!-- Corners (red #f43f5e), Edges (amber #fbbf24), Center 3x3 (sky #38bdf8) -->\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Edge borders -->\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"134\" width=\"130\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"56\" width=\"26\" height=\"78\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <rect x=\"159\" y=\"56\" width=\"26\" height=\"78\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <!-- 4 Front Corners -->\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <rect x=\"159\" y=\"30\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"134\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <rect x=\"159\" y=\"134\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <!-- Grid lines -->\n        <g stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\">\n          <line x1=\"81\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"81\" y2=\"160\" /><line x1=\"107\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"107\" y2=\"160\" />\n          <line x1=\"133\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"133\" y2=\"160\" /><line x1=\"159\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"159\" y2=\"160\" />\n          <line x1=\"55\" y1=\"56\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"56\" /><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"82\" />\n          <line x1=\"55\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"108\" /><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"134\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"134\" />\n          <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"130\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        </g>\n        <!-- Legend -->\n        <rect x=\"18\" y=\"172\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" /><text x=\"32\" y=\"181\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\">3 faces (8)</text>\n        <rect x=\"92\" y=\"172\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" /><text x=\"106\" y=\"181\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\">2 faces (36)</text>\n        <rect x=\"168\" y=\"172\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" /><text x=\"182\" y=\"181\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\">1 face (54)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">5×5×5 Painted Cube: 8 corners (3 faces), 36 edges (2 faces), 54 face-centres (1 face), 27 interior (0 faces)</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.1",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 6
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "14.2",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 7,
                                    "q": "<p>Two cylinders, A and B, are given. The radius of cylinder B is twice that of cylinder A, and the height of cylinder B is half that of cylinder A. Find the ratio of the curved surface area of A to the curved surface area of B. Also find the ratio of the volume of A to the volume of B.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let cylinder A have radius $r$ and height $h$. Cylinder B has radius $2r$ and height $h/2$.<br>\n<strong>Curved Surface Area (CSA):</strong><br>\n$CSA_A = 2\\pi rh$<br>\n$CSA_B = 2\\pi (2r)(h/2) = 2\\pi rh$<br>\nRatio of CSA = $1 : 1$.<br>\n<strong>Volume (V):</strong><br>\n$V_A = \\pi r^2 h$<br>\n$V_B = \\pi (2r)^2 (h/2) = \\pi (4r^2)(h/2) = 2\\pi r^2 h$<br>\nRatio of Volume = $1 : 2$.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"320\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Cylinder A: r=30, h=100 at x=75 -->\n        <ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"140\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.18)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <rect x=\"45\" y=\"40\" width=\"60\" height=\"100\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.1)\" />\n        <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"105\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"40\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"75\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Cylinder A (r, h)</text>\n        <!-- Cylinder B: 2r=60, h/2=50 at x=220 -->\n        <ellipse cx=\"220\" cy=\"140\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.18)\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <rect x=\"160\" y=\"90\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" />\n        <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <ellipse cx=\"220\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#d1fae5\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"220\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Cylinder B (2r, h/2)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">CSA Ratio = 1 : 1, Volume Ratio = 1 : 2</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 8,
                                    "q": "<p>The edge of a cube measures $r$ cm. The largest possible right circular cylinder is cut out of the cube. What do you think is the volume of the cylinder (in cm³)?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The largest cylinder that fits in a cube of edge $r$ will have a diameter equal to the edge length ($2R = r \\Rightarrow R = r/2$) and a height equal to the edge length ($h = r$).<br>\nVolume of cylinder = $\\pi R^2 h = \\pi \\left(\\frac{r}{2}\\right)^2 (r) = \\pi \\left(\\frac{r^2}{4}\\right) (r) = \\frac{\\pi r^3}{4}$ cm³.</p>\n<svg width=\"150\" height=\"150\" viewBox=\"0 0 150 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<rect x=\"25\" y=\"25\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"25\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#d3d3d3\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"25\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"125\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#e0e0e0\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n</svg>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 9,
                                    "q": "<p>The radius of the base of a cylinder is increased by 10%. At the same time, the height of the cylinder is decreased by x%. Given that the volume of the cylinder remains unchanged, find the value of x.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the original radius be $r$ and height be $h$. Volume $V = \\pi r^2 h$.<br>\nNew radius = $1.1r$. New height = $h \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)$.<br>\nNew Volume = $\\pi (1.1r)^2 \\left(h \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)\\right) = 1.21 \\pi r^2 h \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)$.<br>\nSince the volume is unchanged: $1 = 1.21 \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)$.<br>\n$1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{1}{1.21} = \\frac{100}{121}$<br>\n$\\frac{x}{100} = 1 - \\frac{100}{121} = \\frac{21}{121}$<br>\n$x = \\frac{2100}{121} \\approx 17.36\\%$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q5",
                                    "globalIndex": 10,
                                    "q": "<p>A solid metallic cube of side 12 cm is melted and recast into solid cylindrical rods, each having radius 2 cm and height 12 cm. Find:<br>\n(i) the volume of the cube,<br>\n(ii) the volume of one cylindrical rod,<br>\n(iii) the approximate number of complete cylindrical rods that can be formed. ($\\pi \\approx \\frac{22}{7}$)</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n(i) Volume of the cube = $a^3 = 12^3 = 1728$ cm³.<br>\n(ii) Volume of one cylindrical rod = $\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 2^2 \\times 12 = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 48 = \\frac{1056}{7} \\approx 150.85$ cm³.<br>\n(iii) Number of complete rods = $\\frac{\\text{Volume of cube}}{\\text{Volume of one rod}} = \\frac{1728}{150.85} \\approx 11.45$. So, <strong>11</strong> complete cylindrical rods can be formed.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.2",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "14.3",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 11,
                                    "q": "<p>Find the total surface area of a cone, if its slant height is 21 m and diameter of its base is 24 m.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Slant height ($l$) = 21 m. Radius ($r$) = $24/2 = 12$ m.<br>\nTotal Surface Area (TSA) = $\\pi r(l + r)$<br>\nTSA = $\\frac{22}{7} \\times 12 \\times (21 + 12) = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 12 \\times 33 = \\frac{8712}{7} \\approx 1244.57$ m².</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 12,
                                    "q": "<p>The height of a cone is 16 cm, and its base radius is 12 cm. Find the curved surface area and the total surface area of the cone.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Height ($h$) = 16 cm, Radius ($r$) = 12 cm.<br>\nFirst, find slant height ($l$): $l = \\sqrt{h^2 + r^2} = \\sqrt{16^2 + 12^2} = \\sqrt{256 + 144} = \\sqrt{400} = 20$ cm.<br>\nCurved Surface Area (CSA) = $\\pi rl = 3.14 \\times 12 \\times 20 = 753.6$ cm² (or exactly $240\\pi$ cm²).<br>\nTotal Surface Area (TSA) = $\\pi r(l + r) = 3.14 \\times 12 \\times (20 + 12) = 3.14 \\times 12 \\times 32 = 1205.76$ cm² (or exactly $384\\pi$ cm²).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"240\" height=\"205\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 205\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"160\" rx=\"68\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <path d=\"M 52 160 L 120 30 L 188 160\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Height h=16 and radius r=12 -->\n        <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <path d=\"M 120 150 L 130 150 L 130 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n        <text x=\"112\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">h = 16 cm</text>\n        <text x=\"154\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">r = 12 cm</text>\n        <text x=\"165\" y=\"92\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">l = 20 cm</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Right circular cone: Slant height l = √(16² + 12²) = 20 cm</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 13,
                                    "q": "<p>A cone has a height of 15 cm. If its volume is 1570 cm³, find the radius of the base.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume ($V$) = $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h$.<br>\n$1570 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times r^2 \\times 15$<br>\n$1570 = 5 \\times 3.14 \\times r^2$<br>\n$1570 = 15.7 \\times r^2$<br>\n$r^2 = 100 \\Rightarrow r = 10$ cm.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q8",
                                    "globalIndex": 14,
                                    "q": "<p>A right triangle with sides 6 cm, 8 cm and 10 cm is rotated through 360° about the side of 8 cm. Find the volume and the curved surface area of the solid so formed.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Rotating the triangle about the 8 cm side forms a right circular cone with height ($h$) = 8 cm, base radius ($r$) = 6 cm, and slant height ($l$) = 10 cm.<br>\nVolume ($V$) = $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times 36 \\times 8 = 301.44$ cm³ (or exactly $96\\pi$ cm³).<br>\nCurved Surface Area (CSA) = $\\pi rl = 3.14 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 188.4$ cm² (or exactly $60\\pi$ cm²).</p>\n<svg width=\"150\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 150 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<polygon points=\"75,20 75,150 135,150\" fill=\"#add8e6\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"75\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"150\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"90\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">8</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"165\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">6</text>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"80\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">10</text>\n</svg>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.3",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "14.4",
                            "name": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "title": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q1",
                                    "globalIndex": 15,
                                    "q": "<p>A ball bearing has a radius of 0.7 cm. Find its surface area.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A ball bearing is spherical. Surface Area = $4\\pi r^2$.<br>\nSA = $4 \\times \\frac{22}{7} \\times (0.7)^2 = 4 \\times \\frac{22}{7} \\times 0.49 = 4 \\times 22 \\times 0.07 = 6.16$ cm².</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q2",
                                    "globalIndex": 16,
                                    "q": "<p>Two solid spheres made of the same metal have weights 5920 g and 740 g. Determine the radius of the larger sphere, if the diameter of the smaller one is 5 cm.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Since they are made of the same metal, their weights are directly proportional to their volumes. Ratio of volumes = $5920 / 740 = 8$.<br>\nVolume ratio = $(\\text{radius ratio})^3 = \\left(\\frac{R_{large}}{R_{small}}\\right)^3 = 8 \\Rightarrow \\frac{R_{large}}{R_{small}} = 2$.<br>\nThe radius of the smaller sphere = $5 / 2 = 2.5$ cm.<br>\nThe radius of the larger sphere = $2 \\times 2.5 = 5$ cm.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q3",
                                    "globalIndex": 17,
                                    "q": "<p>The diameter of the moon is approximately one fourth the diameter of the earth. Given that the moon and earth are both roughly spherical, find the ratio of their surface areas.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the diameter of the Earth be $D_E$. Then the diameter of the moon is $D_M = \\frac{1}{4} D_E$. Their radii hold the same ratio: $r_M = \\frac{1}{4} r_E$.<br>\nRatio of Surface Areas = $\\frac{4\\pi r_M^2}{4\\pi r_E^2} = \\left(\\frac{r_M}{r_E}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{16}$.<br>\nThe ratio is $1 : 16$.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q5",
                                    "globalIndex": 18,
                                    "q": "<p>Metal spheres, each of radius 2 cm, are packed into a rectangular box of internal dimensions $16 \\text{ cm} \\times 8 \\text{ cm} \\times 8 \\text{ cm}$. When 16 spheres are packed, the box is filled with preservative liquid. Find the volume of this liquid. Round your answer to the nearest integer.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume of the box = $16 \\times 8 \\times 8 = 1024$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of one sphere = $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times (2^3) = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times 8 \\approx 33.51$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of 16 spheres = $16 \\times 33.51 \\approx 536.16$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of preservative liquid = Volume of box - Volume of 16 spheres = $1024 - 536.16 = 487.84$ cm³.<br>\nRounded to the nearest integer, the volume is <strong>488 cm³</strong>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"300\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 165\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Top-down/front cross-section of 16 cm x 8 cm box holding 4x2 spheres (d=4 cm each) -->\n        <rect x=\"40\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"100\" fill=\"rgba(56,189,248,0.2)\" stroke=\"#0284c7\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n        <!-- 8 visible spheres in front layer (4x2), r=25px -->\n        <g fill=\"rgba(100,116,139,0.35)\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n          <circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" />\n          <circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"215\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" />\n          <circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" />\n          <circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"215\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" />\n        </g>\n        <text x=\"140\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">Length = 16 cm (4 spheres × 4 cm)</text>\n        <text x=\"248\" y=\"80\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0284c7\">8 cm</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">16 spheres (r = 2 cm) arranged 4 × 2 × 2 inside the 16 cm × 8 cm × 8 cm box</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "14.4",
                                    "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "id": "end-of-chapter",
                            "name": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "title": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "questions": [
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q4",
                                    "globalIndex": 19,
                                    "q": "<p>You are given two options: (A) one chocolate cube of side 50 mm, (B) hundred chocolate cubes of side 10 mm. Which option gives you more chocolate?</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <br>\nOption A Volume = $a^3 = 50^3 = 125,000$ mm³.<br>\nOption B Volume = $100 \\times (10^3) = 100 \\times 1000 = 100,000$ mm³.<br>\n<strong>Option A</strong> gives you more chocolate.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 0
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q6",
                                    "globalIndex": 20,
                                    "q": "<p>A school provides milk to its students in cylindrical glasses, each with a diameter of 7 cm. If each glass is filled with milk to a height of 12 cm, how many litres of milk are needed to serve 1600 students? Use 1000 cm³ = 1 litre.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Radius of the glass ($r$) = $7 / 2 = 3.5$ cm. Height ($h$) = 12 cm.<br>\nVolume of milk per student = $\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 3.5^2 \\times 12 = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 12.25 \\times 12 = 462$ cm³.<br>\nTotal volume for 1600 students = $1600 \\times 462 = 739,200$ cm³.<br>\nVolume in litres = $\\frac{739,200}{1000} = 739.2$ litres.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 1
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q7",
                                    "globalIndex": 21,
                                    "q": "<p>The surface area of a sphere of radius 5 cm is five times the area of the curved surface of a cone of radius 4 cm. Find the height and the volume of the cone.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Surface area of sphere ($r_s=5$) = $4\\pi (5^2) = 100\\pi$.<br>\nCurved surface area (CSA) of cone ($r_c=4$) = $\\pi(4)l$.<br>\nGiven: $100\\pi = 5 \\times (4\\pi l) \\Rightarrow 100\\pi = 20\\pi l \\Rightarrow l = 5$ cm.<br>\nHeight of cone ($h$) = $\\sqrt{l^2 - r_c^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = 3$ cm.<br>\nVolume of the cone = $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r_c^2 h = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\pi \\times (4^2) \\times 3 = 16\\pi \\approx 50.24$ cm³.</p>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 2
                                },
                                {
                                    "qNum": "Q11",
                                    "globalIndex": 22,
                                    "q": "<p>We have a cylinder with a base radius of r cm and height h cm. A square pyramid is fitted inside it. The square base of the pyramid lies on the base of the cylinder, its corners on the boundary of the cylinder. The apex of the pyramid lies on the top of the cylinder. Find the ratio of the volume of this pyramid to the volume of the cylinder.</p>",
                                    "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the square base of the pyramid have side $a$. Its diagonal connects opposite corners on the circle's boundary, so the diagonal = $2r$.<br>\nBy Pythagoras, $a^2 + a^2 = (2r)^2 \\Rightarrow 2a^2 = 4r^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 2r^2$. Thus, the area of the square base = $2r^2$.<br>\nThe height of the pyramid is $h$.<br>\nVolume of the pyramid = $\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Area of base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{3}(2r^2)h = \\frac{2}{3}r^2 h$.<br>\nVolume of the cylinder = $\\pi r^2 h$.<br>\nRatio = $\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}r^2 h}{\\pi r^2 h} = \\frac{2}{3\\pi}$. Thus, the ratio is $2 : 3\\pi$.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"215\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 215\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Cylinder top & bottom ellipses -->\n        <ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"165\" rx=\"75\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"55\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"40\" rx=\"75\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Inscribed square base on bottom ellipse: (55,165), (130,187), (205,165), (130,143) -->\n        <polygon points=\"55,165 130,187 205,165 130,143\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.22)\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Pyramid edges to apex (130, 40) -->\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"187\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"143\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"40\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"130\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Apex</text>\n        <text x=\"130\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Square base (Diagonal = 2r, Area = 2r²)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Square pyramid inscribed in a cylinder: Volume Ratio = (2/3 r²h) / (πr²h) = 2 : 3π</div>\n</div>",
                                    "hint": "",
                                    "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                                    "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                                    "exQuestionIndex": 3
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ],
                    "solutions": [
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 0,
                            "q": "<p>The volume of a cube is 64 cm³. What is its total surface area?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the side of the cube be $a$.<br>\nVolume = $a^3 = 64 \\Rightarrow a = 4$ cm.<br>\nThe Total Surface Area (TSA) of a cube is $6a^2$.<br>\nTSA = $6(4^2) = 6(16) = 96$ cm².</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 1,
                            "q": "<p>How many small cubes with side 20 cm can be packed tight in a cubical box with side 2 m?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> First, convert the side of the large box to cm: $2$ m = $200$ cm.<br>\nVolume of the large box = $(200)^3 = 8,000,000$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of one small cube = $(20)^3 = 8,000$ cm³.<br>\nNumber of small cubes = $\\frac{8,000,000}{8,000} = 1000$ cubes.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 2,
                            "q": "<p>The dimensions of a godown are $40 \\text{ m} \\times 25 \\text{ m} \\times 10 \\text{ m}$. If it is filled with cuboidal boxes, each of dimensions $2 \\text{ m} \\times 1.25 \\text{ m} \\times 1 \\text{ m}$, then find the number of boxes.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume of the godown = $40 \\times 25 \\times 10 = 10,000$ m³.<br>\nVolume of one box = $2 \\times 1.25 \\times 1 = 2.5$ m³.<br>\nNumber of boxes = $\\frac{10,000}{2.5} = 4000$ boxes.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 3,
                            "q": "<p>Two cubes each of volume 125 cm³ are joined end to end. Find the surface area of the resulting cuboid.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume of one cube = $a^3 = 125 \\Rightarrow a = 5$ cm.<br>\nWhen joined end to end, the resulting cuboid has:<br>\nLength ($l$) = $5 + 5 = 10$ cm<br>\nWidth ($w$) = $5$ cm<br>\nHeight ($h$) = $5$ cm<br>\nTotal Surface Area = $2(lw + wh + hl) = 2((10)(5) + (5)(5) + (5)(10)) = 2(50 + 25 + 50) = 2(125) = 250$ cm².</p>\n<svg width=\"200\" height=\"100\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#e0e0e0\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#c0c0c0\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\"/>\n<text x=\"55\" y=\"55\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">5</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"55\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">5</text>\n</svg>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q5",
                            "globalIndex": 4,
                            "q": "<p>A cube of side 4 cm is cut into cubes of side 1 cm. What is the ratio of the surface areas of the original cube and all the cut-out cubes?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Surface area of the original cube = $6(4^2) = 6(16) = 96$ cm².<br>\nVolume of the original cube = $4^3 = 64$ cm³, which yields 64 smaller cubes of 1 cm³ each.<br>\nSurface area of one 1 cm cube = $6(1^2) = 6$ cm².<br>\nTotal surface area of all 64 smaller cubes = $64 \\times 6 = 384$ cm².<br>\nRatio = $96 : 384 = 1 : 4$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 4
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q6",
                            "globalIndex": 5,
                            "q": "<p>The surface areas of the three faces of a cuboid that meet at one of the corners of the cuboid are 6 cm², 15 cm², and 10 cm² respectively. What is the volume of the cuboid?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the dimensions be $l, w, h$.<br>\nGiven: $lw = 6$, $wh = 15$, $lh = 10$.<br>\nMultiply all three equations: $(lw)(wh)(lh) = 6 \\times 15 \\times 10$<br>\n$(lwh)^2 = 900$<br>\nVolume $V = lwh = \\sqrt{900} = 30$ cm³.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Front face (lh = 10 cm^2) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,70 165,70 165,150 45,150\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.15)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Top face (lw = 6 cm^2) -->\n        <polygon points=\"45,70 90,35 210,35 165,70\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.18)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Right face (wh = 15 cm^2) -->\n        <polygon points=\"165,70 210,35 210,115 165,150\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.18)\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"105\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\">lh = 10 cm²</text>\n        <text x=\"128\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">lw = 6 cm²</text>\n        <text x=\"188\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b45309\">wh=15</text>\n        <text x=\"105\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">V = √(6 × 15 × 10) = 30 cm³</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Cuboid with three concurrent face areas lw = 6 cm², wh = 15 cm², lh = 10 cm²</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 5
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q7",
                            "globalIndex": 6,
                            "q": "<p>A cube of side 5 cm is painted on all its faces. If it is sliced into 1 cm³ cubes, how many of these 1 cm³ cubes have:<br>\n(i) exactly three faces painted?<br>\n(ii) exactly two faces painted?<br>\n(iii) exactly one face painted?<br>\n(iv) no face painted?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Total smaller cubes = $5 \\times 5 \\times 5 = 125$.<br>\n(i) Exactly three faces painted (corners): <strong>8</strong> cubes.<br>\n(ii) Exactly two faces painted (edges, excluding corners): 12 edges $\\times$ (5 - 2) = $12 \\times 3 = $ <strong>36</strong> cubes.<br>\n(iii) Exactly one face painted (centers of faces): 6 faces $\\times$ (3 $\\times$ 3) = $6 \\times 9 = $ <strong>54</strong> cubes.<br>\n(iv) No face painted (interior cubes): $(5 - 2) \\times (5 - 2) \\times (5 - 2) = 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = $ <strong>27</strong> cubes.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"240\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 200\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- 5x5 front face grid at (55, 30), cell=26px -> 130x130 -->\n        <!-- Corners (red #f43f5e), Edges (amber #fbbf24), Center 3x3 (sky #38bdf8) -->\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"130\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Edge borders -->\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"134\" width=\"130\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"56\" width=\"26\" height=\"78\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <rect x=\"159\" y=\"56\" width=\"26\" height=\"78\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" />\n        <!-- 4 Front Corners -->\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <rect x=\"159\" y=\"30\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <rect x=\"55\" y=\"134\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <rect x=\"159\" y=\"134\" width=\"26\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" />\n        <!-- Grid lines -->\n        <g stroke=\"#1e293b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\">\n          <line x1=\"81\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"81\" y2=\"160\" /><line x1=\"107\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"107\" y2=\"160\" />\n          <line x1=\"133\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"133\" y2=\"160\" /><line x1=\"159\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"159\" y2=\"160\" />\n          <line x1=\"55\" y1=\"56\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"56\" /><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"82\" />\n          <line x1=\"55\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"108\" /><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"134\" x2=\"185\" y2=\"134\" />\n          <rect x=\"55\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"130\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        </g>\n        <!-- Legend -->\n        <rect x=\"18\" y=\"172\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#f43f5e\" /><text x=\"32\" y=\"181\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\">3 faces (8)</text>\n        <rect x=\"92\" y=\"172\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" /><text x=\"106\" y=\"181\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\">2 faces (36)</text>\n        <rect x=\"168\" y=\"172\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" /><text x=\"182\" y=\"181\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#1e293b\">1 face (54)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">5×5×5 Painted Cube: 8 corners (3 faces), 36 edges (2 faces), 54 face-centres (1 face), 27 interior (0 faces)</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.1",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.1",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 6
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 7,
                            "q": "<p>Two cylinders, A and B, are given. The radius of cylinder B is twice that of cylinder A, and the height of cylinder B is half that of cylinder A. Find the ratio of the curved surface area of A to the curved surface area of B. Also find the ratio of the volume of A to the volume of B.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let cylinder A have radius $r$ and height $h$. Cylinder B has radius $2r$ and height $h/2$.<br>\n<strong>Curved Surface Area (CSA):</strong><br>\n$CSA_A = 2\\pi rh$<br>\n$CSA_B = 2\\pi (2r)(h/2) = 2\\pi rh$<br>\nRatio of CSA = $1 : 1$.<br>\n<strong>Volume (V):</strong><br>\n$V_A = \\pi r^2 h$<br>\n$V_B = \\pi (2r)^2 (h/2) = \\pi (4r^2)(h/2) = 2\\pi r^2 h$<br>\nRatio of Volume = $1 : 2$.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"320\" height=\"185\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 185\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Cylinder A: r=30, h=100 at x=75 -->\n        <ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"140\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.18)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <rect x=\"45\" y=\"40\" width=\"60\" height=\"100\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.1)\" />\n        <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"105\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"40\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"75\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Cylinder A (r, h)</text>\n        <!-- Cylinder B: 2r=60, h/2=50 at x=220 -->\n        <ellipse cx=\"220\" cy=\"140\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.18)\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <rect x=\"160\" y=\"90\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.1)\" />\n        <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"280\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <ellipse cx=\"220\" cy=\"90\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#d1fae5\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <text x=\"220\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Cylinder B (2r, h/2)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">CSA Ratio = 1 : 1, Volume Ratio = 1 : 2</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 8,
                            "q": "<p>The edge of a cube measures $r$ cm. The largest possible right circular cylinder is cut out of the cube. What do you think is the volume of the cylinder (in cm³)?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The largest cylinder that fits in a cube of edge $r$ will have a diameter equal to the edge length ($2R = r \\Rightarrow R = r/2$) and a height equal to the edge length ($h = r$).<br>\nVolume of cylinder = $\\pi R^2 h = \\pi \\left(\\frac{r}{2}\\right)^2 (r) = \\pi \\left(\\frac{r^2}{4}\\right) (r) = \\frac{\\pi r^3}{4}$ cm³.</p>\n<svg width=\"150\" height=\"150\" viewBox=\"0 0 150 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<rect x=\"25\" y=\"25\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"25\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#d3d3d3\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"25\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"125\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#e0e0e0\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n</svg>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 9,
                            "q": "<p>The radius of the base of a cylinder is increased by 10%. At the same time, the height of the cylinder is decreased by x%. Given that the volume of the cylinder remains unchanged, find the value of x.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the original radius be $r$ and height be $h$. Volume $V = \\pi r^2 h$.<br>\nNew radius = $1.1r$. New height = $h \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)$.<br>\nNew Volume = $\\pi (1.1r)^2 \\left(h \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)\\right) = 1.21 \\pi r^2 h \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)$.<br>\nSince the volume is unchanged: $1 = 1.21 \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right)$.<br>\n$1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{1}{1.21} = \\frac{100}{121}$<br>\n$\\frac{x}{100} = 1 - \\frac{100}{121} = \\frac{21}{121}$<br>\n$x = \\frac{2100}{121} \\approx 17.36\\%$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q5",
                            "globalIndex": 10,
                            "q": "<p>A solid metallic cube of side 12 cm is melted and recast into solid cylindrical rods, each having radius 2 cm and height 12 cm. Find:<br>\n(i) the volume of the cube,<br>\n(ii) the volume of one cylindrical rod,<br>\n(iii) the approximate number of complete cylindrical rods that can be formed. ($\\pi \\approx \\frac{22}{7}$)</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n(i) Volume of the cube = $a^3 = 12^3 = 1728$ cm³.<br>\n(ii) Volume of one cylindrical rod = $\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 2^2 \\times 12 = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 48 = \\frac{1056}{7} \\approx 150.85$ cm³.<br>\n(iii) Number of complete rods = $\\frac{\\text{Volume of cube}}{\\text{Volume of one rod}} = \\frac{1728}{150.85} \\approx 11.45$. So, <strong>11</strong> complete cylindrical rods can be formed.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.2",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.2",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 11,
                            "q": "<p>Find the total surface area of a cone, if its slant height is 21 m and diameter of its base is 24 m.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Slant height ($l$) = 21 m. Radius ($r$) = $24/2 = 12$ m.<br>\nTotal Surface Area (TSA) = $\\pi r(l + r)$<br>\nTSA = $\\frac{22}{7} \\times 12 \\times (21 + 12) = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 12 \\times 33 = \\frac{8712}{7} \\approx 1244.57$ m².</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 12,
                            "q": "<p>The height of a cone is 16 cm, and its base radius is 12 cm. Find the curved surface area and the total surface area of the cone.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Height ($h$) = 16 cm, Radius ($r$) = 12 cm.<br>\nFirst, find slant height ($l$): $l = \\sqrt{h^2 + r^2} = \\sqrt{16^2 + 12^2} = \\sqrt{256 + 144} = \\sqrt{400} = 20$ cm.<br>\nCurved Surface Area (CSA) = $\\pi rl = 3.14 \\times 12 \\times 20 = 753.6$ cm² (or exactly $240\\pi$ cm²).<br>\nTotal Surface Area (TSA) = $\\pi r(l + r) = 3.14 \\times 12 \\times (20 + 12) = 3.14 \\times 12 \\times 32 = 1205.76$ cm² (or exactly $384\\pi$ cm²).</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"240\" height=\"205\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 205\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"160\" rx=\"68\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <path d=\"M 52 160 L 120 30 L 188 160\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2.2\" />\n        <!-- Height h=16 and radius r=12 -->\n        <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <line x1=\"120\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <path d=\"M 120 150 L 130 150 L 130 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" />\n        <text x=\"112\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">h = 16 cm</text>\n        <text x=\"154\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">r = 12 cm</text>\n        <text x=\"165\" y=\"92\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2563eb\">l = 20 cm</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Right circular cone: Slant height l = √(16² + 12²) = 20 cm</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 13,
                            "q": "<p>A cone has a height of 15 cm. If its volume is 1570 cm³, find the radius of the base.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume ($V$) = $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h$.<br>\n$1570 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times r^2 \\times 15$<br>\n$1570 = 5 \\times 3.14 \\times r^2$<br>\n$1570 = 15.7 \\times r^2$<br>\n$r^2 = 100 \\Rightarrow r = 10$ cm.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q8",
                            "globalIndex": 14,
                            "q": "<p>A right triangle with sides 6 cm, 8 cm and 10 cm is rotated through 360° about the side of 8 cm. Find the volume and the curved surface area of the solid so formed.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Rotating the triangle about the 8 cm side forms a right circular cone with height ($h$) = 8 cm, base radius ($r$) = 6 cm, and slant height ($l$) = 10 cm.<br>\nVolume ($V$) = $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times 36 \\times 8 = 301.44$ cm³ (or exactly $96\\pi$ cm³).<br>\nCurved Surface Area (CSA) = $\\pi rl = 3.14 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 188.4$ cm² (or exactly $60\\pi$ cm²).</p>\n<svg width=\"150\" height=\"200\" viewBox=\"0 0 150 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n<polygon points=\"75,20 75,150 135,150\" fill=\"#add8e6\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<line x1=\"75\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"75\" cy=\"150\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"90\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">8</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"165\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">6</text>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"80\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\">10</text>\n</svg>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.3",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.3",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q1",
                            "globalIndex": 15,
                            "q": "<p>A ball bearing has a radius of 0.7 cm. Find its surface area.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A ball bearing is spherical. Surface Area = $4\\pi r^2$.<br>\nSA = $4 \\times \\frac{22}{7} \\times (0.7)^2 = 4 \\times \\frac{22}{7} \\times 0.49 = 4 \\times 22 \\times 0.07 = 6.16$ cm².</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q2",
                            "globalIndex": 16,
                            "q": "<p>Two solid spheres made of the same metal have weights 5920 g and 740 g. Determine the radius of the larger sphere, if the diameter of the smaller one is 5 cm.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Since they are made of the same metal, their weights are directly proportional to their volumes. Ratio of volumes = $5920 / 740 = 8$.<br>\nVolume ratio = $(\\text{radius ratio})^3 = \\left(\\frac{R_{large}}{R_{small}}\\right)^3 = 8 \\Rightarrow \\frac{R_{large}}{R_{small}} = 2$.<br>\nThe radius of the smaller sphere = $5 / 2 = 2.5$ cm.<br>\nThe radius of the larger sphere = $2 \\times 2.5 = 5$ cm.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q3",
                            "globalIndex": 17,
                            "q": "<p>The diameter of the moon is approximately one fourth the diameter of the earth. Given that the moon and earth are both roughly spherical, find the ratio of their surface areas.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the diameter of the Earth be $D_E$. Then the diameter of the moon is $D_M = \\frac{1}{4} D_E$. Their radii hold the same ratio: $r_M = \\frac{1}{4} r_E$.<br>\nRatio of Surface Areas = $\\frac{4\\pi r_M^2}{4\\pi r_E^2} = \\left(\\frac{r_M}{r_E}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{16}$.<br>\nThe ratio is $1 : 16$.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q5",
                            "globalIndex": 18,
                            "q": "<p>Metal spheres, each of radius 2 cm, are packed into a rectangular box of internal dimensions $16 \\text{ cm} \\times 8 \\text{ cm} \\times 8 \\text{ cm}$. When 16 spheres are packed, the box is filled with preservative liquid. Find the volume of this liquid. Round your answer to the nearest integer.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Volume of the box = $16 \\times 8 \\times 8 = 1024$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of one sphere = $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times (2^3) = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times 8 \\approx 33.51$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of 16 spheres = $16 \\times 33.51 \\approx 536.16$ cm³.<br>\nVolume of preservative liquid = Volume of box - Volume of 16 spheres = $1024 - 536.16 = 487.84$ cm³.<br>\nRounded to the nearest integer, the volume is <strong>488 cm³</strong>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"300\" height=\"165\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 165\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Top-down/front cross-section of 16 cm x 8 cm box holding 4x2 spheres (d=4 cm each) -->\n        <rect x=\"40\" y=\"25\" width=\"200\" height=\"100\" fill=\"rgba(56,189,248,0.2)\" stroke=\"#0284c7\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" />\n        <!-- 8 visible spheres in front layer (4x2), r=25px -->\n        <g fill=\"rgba(100,116,139,0.35)\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n          <circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" />\n          <circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"215\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" />\n          <circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" />\n          <circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" /><circle cx=\"215\" cy=\"100\" r=\"24\" />\n        </g>\n        <text x=\"140\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0f172a\">Length = 16 cm (4 spheres × 4 cm)</text>\n        <text x=\"248\" y=\"80\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#0284c7\">8 cm</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">16 spheres (r = 2 cm) arranged 4 × 2 × 2 inside the 16 cm × 8 cm × 8 cm box</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "14.4",
                            "exerciseName": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise Set 14.4",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q4",
                            "globalIndex": 19,
                            "q": "<p>You are given two options: (A) one chocolate cube of side 50 mm, (B) hundred chocolate cubes of side 10 mm. Which option gives you more chocolate?</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <br>\nOption A Volume = $a^3 = 50^3 = 125,000$ mm³.<br>\nOption B Volume = $100 \\times (10^3) = 100 \\times 1000 = 100,000$ mm³.<br>\n<strong>Option A</strong> gives you more chocolate.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 0
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q6",
                            "globalIndex": 20,
                            "q": "<p>A school provides milk to its students in cylindrical glasses, each with a diameter of 7 cm. If each glass is filled with milk to a height of 12 cm, how many litres of milk are needed to serve 1600 students? Use 1000 cm³ = 1 litre.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Radius of the glass ($r$) = $7 / 2 = 3.5$ cm. Height ($h$) = 12 cm.<br>\nVolume of milk per student = $\\pi r^2 h = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 3.5^2 \\times 12 = \\frac{22}{7} \\times 12.25 \\times 12 = 462$ cm³.<br>\nTotal volume for 1600 students = $1600 \\times 462 = 739,200$ cm³.<br>\nVolume in litres = $\\frac{739,200}{1000} = 739.2$ litres.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 1
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q7",
                            "globalIndex": 21,
                            "q": "<p>The surface area of a sphere of radius 5 cm is five times the area of the curved surface of a cone of radius 4 cm. Find the height and the volume of the cone.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Surface area of sphere ($r_s=5$) = $4\\pi (5^2) = 100\\pi$.<br>\nCurved surface area (CSA) of cone ($r_c=4$) = $\\pi(4)l$.<br>\nGiven: $100\\pi = 5 \\times (4\\pi l) \\Rightarrow 100\\pi = 20\\pi l \\Rightarrow l = 5$ cm.<br>\nHeight of cone ($h$) = $\\sqrt{l^2 - r_c^2} = \\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = \\sqrt{25 - 16} = 3$ cm.<br>\nVolume of the cone = $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r_c^2 h = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\pi \\times (4^2) \\times 3 = 16\\pi \\approx 50.24$ cm³.</p>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 2
                        },
                        {
                            "qNum": "Q11",
                            "globalIndex": 22,
                            "q": "<p>We have a cylinder with a base radius of r cm and height h cm. A square pyramid is fitted inside it. The square base of the pyramid lies on the base of the cylinder, its corners on the boundary of the cylinder. The apex of the pyramid lies on the top of the cylinder. Find the ratio of the volume of this pyramid to the volume of the cylinder.</p>",
                            "a": "<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Let the square base of the pyramid have side $a$. Its diagonal connects opposite corners on the circle's boundary, so the diagonal = $2r$.<br>\nBy Pythagoras, $a^2 + a^2 = (2r)^2 \\Rightarrow 2a^2 = 4r^2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 2r^2$. Thus, the area of the square base = $2r^2$.<br>\nThe height of the pyramid is $h$.<br>\nVolume of the pyramid = $\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{Area of base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{3}(2r^2)h = \\frac{2}{3}r^2 h$.<br>\nVolume of the cylinder = $\\pi r^2 h$.<br>\nRatio = $\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}r^2 h}{\\pi r^2 h} = \\frac{2}{3\\pi}$. Thus, the ratio is $2 : 3\\pi$.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 14px 0;\">\n  <svg width=\"260\" height=\"215\" viewBox=\"0 0 260 215\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:8px;\">\n        <!-- Cylinder top & bottom ellipses -->\n        <ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"165\" rx=\"75\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"55\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"205\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"40\" rx=\"75\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(59,130,246,0.08)\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Inscribed square base on bottom ellipse: (55,165), (130,187), (205,165), (130,143) -->\n        <polygon points=\"55,165 130,187 205,165 130,143\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.22)\" stroke=\"#047857\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <!-- Pyramid edges to apex (130, 40) -->\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"187\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"205\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n        <line x1=\"130\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"143\" stroke=\"#e11d48\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" />\n        <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"40\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#e11d48\" />\n        <text x=\"130\" y=\"25\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e11d48\">Apex</text>\n        <text x=\"130\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#047857\">Square base (Diagonal = 2r, Area = 2r²)</text>\n      </svg>\n  <div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; margin-top: 6px; font-style: italic;\">Square pyramid inscribed in a cylinder: Volume Ratio = (2/3 r²h) / (πr²h) = 2 : 3π</div>\n</div>",
                            "hint": "",
                            "exerciseId": "end-of-chapter",
                            "exerciseName": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exerciseTitle": "End-of-Chapter Exercises",
                            "exQuestionIndex": 3
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
},
    sst: {
        title: "Social Science Section" ,
        isBranching: true,
        branches: [ 
            {
                name: "Part 1 - Geography",
                chapters: [
                    { name: "Chapter 1: Understanding Social Science", hasNoExercises: true, solutions: [{ q: "Introductory Module", a: `This chapter is an introductory orientation module in the NCERT Class 9 textbook and contains no end-of-chapter exercises.` }] },
                    { name: "Chapter 2: Shaping of the Earth’s Surface", solutions: [
                        { 
                            q: "What are the sources of energy that are required to cause movements associated with the internal forces of the Earth?", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Core Heat Source:</strong> The movements driven by the Earth's internal forces are powered by intense heat originating from the planet's core.</li>
    <li><strong>Convection Mechanism:</strong> This extreme heat warms the molten material within the mantle, creating massive, continuous convection currents where hot material rises and cooler material sinks. These strong thermal currents act as a subterranean engine that pushes and pulls the tectonic plates, causing the outer crust to shift.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Relate various physiographic divisions you have studied in the earlier grades with various endogenic forces responsible for their origin.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Mountain Building:</strong> Internal or endogenic forces operating within the Earth—specifically plate movements like folding and faulting—are directly responsible for creating major macro-landforms such as mountains, plains, and valleys.</li>
    <li><strong>Tectonic Collisions:</strong> For instance, when two massive continental landmasses move toward each other at a convergent boundary, the immense pressure buckles the crust. This specific endogenic force drives the formation of majestic fold mountain ranges, a prime example being the Himalaya.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Why and where do earthquakes occur frequently? Is it possible to predict earthquakes?", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Geographic Distribution:</strong> Earthquakes occur frequently along plate boundaries—the edges where tectonic plates meet and slide past, pull apart, or crash into one another. A vast majority of these seismic events take place around the Pacific Ocean in a highly active region famously known as the Ring of Fire.</li>
    <li><strong>Predictability Limitations:</strong> While modern geologists study plate maps to pinpoint high-risk zones and help cities manage potential disasters, exactly predicting their timing remains incredibly difficult. Historically, scholars like Varāhamihira attempted to track environmental clues like animal behaviour, clouds, and wind changes to signal them, reflecting an early attempt to blend observation with cosmological reasoning.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: `"Plate movements are responsible for the distribution of earthquakes and volcanoes." Explain.`, 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Boundary Dynamics:</strong> Tectonic plates are in a state of slow, constant motion driven by mantle convection. The intense friction, pressure, and structural cracking that happen where these massive slabs of solid rock interact create the perfect conditions for geological instability.</li>
    <li><strong>Seismic Realities:</strong> When plates pull apart at divergent boundaries, magma forces its way up to create new crust, and when plates collide at convergent boundaries, tectonic activity triggers severe volcanic eruptions and earthquakes. Because these violent releases of sub-crustal energy are concentrated right along the margins where plates grind together, most of the world's volcanoes and earthquakes align perfectly with plate borders.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Draw and label a diagram of a meander and a delta.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Meander and Delta Features:</strong> A meander is a winding curve or loop formed in the middle or lower course of a river due to lateral erosion on outer banks and sediment deposition on inner banks. A delta is a fan-shaped or triangular landform created at a river's mouth where it slows down, enters a sea or lake, and deposits its remaining load.</li>
    <li><strong>Visual Presentation:</strong> The structural configurations, layout, and labeling criteria for both of these water-formed landscape modifications are illustrated below.</li>
</ul>
<div style="margin-top: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #fafafa; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #eee;">
    <img src="images/aa.png" alt="Meander and Delta Diagram" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0 auto; border-radius: 4px;">
</div>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "How are deforestation and erosion associated with each other? Explain.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Loss of Soil Anchor:</strong> Deforestation severely accelerates surface erosion by removing the vital root networks that naturally anchor the soil together. Without this protective root system and vegetation cover, the raw land is left completely exposed and vulnerable.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental Domino Effect:</strong> Once the vegetation is stripped away, natural forces like heavy rainfall and strong winds can easily wear down and sweep the fertile topsoil away. This lack of root stability on hillsides and slopes noticeably increases the risk of sudden natural disasters like landslides.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Develop a plan to protect the land in your local area from erosion.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Afforestation and Vegetative Cover:</strong> Plant native trees, shrubs, and grasses along exposed soil surfaces, roadsides, and riverbanks to establish root networks that bind soil particles and curb water runoff.</li>
    <li><strong>Terracing and Bunding on Slopes:</strong> Implement step-terracing and earthen embankments along natural contour lines on elevated ground to slow down surface runoff velocity and enhance groundwater absorption.</li>
    <li><strong>Proper Drainage Systems and Check Dams:</strong> Build structured runoff channels and check dams across gullies to minimize the gouging power of rainwater and trap dislodged topsoil.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Which disasters do you think you might experience in your region? Discuss a mitigation plan in your classroom.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Regional Disaster Identification:</strong> Regions along river basins and coastal belts frequently experience flash floods and storm surges, hilly terrains are prone to landslides, and arid regions regularly encounter dust storms.</li>
    <li><strong>Classroom Mitigation Strategy:</strong> Establish community early warning systems, organize regular evacuation drills, avoid unplanned construction along unstable slopes or flood lines, and construct robust protective structures such as embankments and check dams.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Prepare a model of landforms created by underground water.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Core Landforms to Model:</strong> Illustrate a Karst limestone landscape displaying sinkholes/dolines on the surface crust, hollow cavern chambers underneath, stalactites hanging from the ceiling, stalagmites rising from the floor, joined limestone pillars, and subterranean rivers.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggested Materials:</strong> Use clay, papier-mâché, or plaster of Paris mounted inside an open cardboard box to reveal both the surface collapse holes and the internal cavern formations.</li>
</ul>
<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; margin-top: 15px; align-items: center;">
    <img src="images/braindead.jpg" alt="Cave Landforms" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 4px;">
    <img src="images/headache.jpg" alt="Underground River" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 4px;">
</div>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "What precautionary measures will you take if you are staying in an earthquake-prone region?", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Structural & Home Preparedness:</strong> Construct buildings following earthquake-resistant architectural codes, firmly bolt heavy furniture and appliances to walls, and maintain an emergency kit stocked with first-aid essentials, water, and flashlights.</li>
    <li><strong>During and Immediate Response:</strong> Practice the "Drop, Cover, and Hold On" procedure under sturdy tables away from glass windows and power lines; if outdoors, move directly into open spaces away from tall buildings, bridges, and electric poles.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Prepare a map showing landform-associated disasters that happened in the current calendar year.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Mapping Procedure:</strong> Acquire an outline map of India or the world and categorize recent disaster incidents using distinct color-coded markers or symbols (e.g., brown triangles for landslides, blue symbols for GLOFs/floods, red dots for earthquakes, and yellow bands for dust storms).</li>
    <li><strong>Required Information:</strong> Collect verified disaster reports from current news sources detailing the location, date, affected landform type, and recorded impact.</li>
</ul>
<div style="margin-top: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #fafafa; padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #eee;">
    <img src="images/earthquakes-volcanoes.png" alt="Map showing the distribution of earthquakes and volcanoes" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0 auto; border-radius: 4px; box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);">
    <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.85rem; color: #64748b; font-style: italic;">Fig. 2.4: Reference map showing distribution of earthquake origins and volcanic activity along tectonic boundaries.</p>
</div>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Create a poster showing landforms that are considered to be sacred or important in your region, and add the folk stories associated with them.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>1. Sacred Mountain Summits (Himalayan Fold Mountains - Mt. Kailash & Nanda Devi):</strong> Revered across Himalayan folklore as the celestial axis and the cosmic abode of Lord Shiva and Goddess Parvati. Local folklore tells that the snow-clad peaks stand as divine guardians safeguarding the northern plains from freezing winds. Indigenous communities perform circumambulations (Parikrama) and consider scaling these summits forbidden, a tradition that historically protected fragile high-altitude alpine ecology from human encroachment.</li>
    <li><strong>2. Holy River Confluence (Sangam at Prayagraj - Fluvial River Plains):</strong> In folklore and epic tradition, this is the sacred meeting point where the Ganga, Yamuna, and mythical subterranean Saraswati converge. According to the <em>Samudra Manthan</em> folk legend, celestial drops of the nectar of immortality (<em>Amrita</em>) spilled into the river here. The visibly contrasting shades—the deep emerald of the Yamuna and the silt-laden ochre of the Ganga—symbolize the harmony of natural elements, inspiring annual gatherings like the Magh and Kumbh Mela centered on river purification.</li>
    <li><strong>3. Sacred Limestone Caverns (Karst Cave Formations - Amarnath & Gupteshwar Caves):</strong> Formed deep within carbonate rock by the persistent action of underground water dissolving and redepositing calcium carbonate into stalactites and stalagmites. In regional folklore, Lord Shiva chose this secluded cave to narrate the secret of creation and immortality (<em>Amar Katha</em>) to Goddess Parvati, where ice stalagmites form natural sacred icons each year. These legends fostered deep respect for subterranean aquifers and sacred springs among local communities.</li>
    <li><strong>4. Community Conservation Value:</strong> Connecting sacred folklore with distinctive geological landforms is one of the world's oldest forms of community-led environmental stewardship, safeguarding mountains, riverbanks, and forests through cultural reverence.</li>
</ul>
<div style="margin-top: 16px; text-align: center; background-color: #fafafa; padding: 14px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;">
    <img src="images/sacred-landforms-poster.jpg" alt="Poster: Sacred Landforms of India" style="max-width: 520px; width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0 auto; border-radius: 6px; box-shadow: 0 4px 14px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);">
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #334155; font-weight: 700;">
        🎨 Student Exhibition Poster: Sacred Landforms of India & Associated Folk Traditions
    </p>
    <p style="margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.8rem; color: #64748b; font-style: italic;">
        Depicting sacred Himalayan snow peaks with prayer flags, the holy river confluence (Sangam) with prayer ghats and floating lamps, and illuminated karst cave formations.
    </p>
</div>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Document a case of a disaster that hit your region in the past, highlighting its effects on various human activities.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Disaster Case Profile (Chamoli Flash Flood, Uttarakhand):</strong>
        <br>&bull; <em>Event & Date:</em> The Chamoli Disaster occurred on 7 February 2021 in the Garhwal Himalayas of Uttarakhand.
        <br>&bull; <em>Location & Affected River Valleys:</em> Chamoli district, surging violently down the catchment basins of the Rishiganga, Dhauliganga, and Alaknanda rivers.
        <br>&bull; <em>Physical Trigger:</em> A massive rock and ice mass detached from the steep slope of Ronti peak (near Nanda Devi), crashing down into the valley floor. The frictional heating rapidly melted glacial ice, transforming into a high-velocity, hyper-concentrated debris flow of ice, water, mud, and gigantic boulders.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Impact on Human Activities:</strong>
        <br>&bull; <em>1. Damage to Transport & Infrastructure:</em> The flash flood washed away five crucial bridges, including the vital border road bridge near Raini village connecting Joshimath to Malari along the Indo-Tibetan border. Severed roads cut off around 13 border villages, paralyzing vehicular movement and supply deliveries.
        <br>&bull; <em>2. Paralyzed Energy & Power Grids:</em> The debris torrent obliterated the 13.2 MW Rishiganga Hydroelectric Project and inflicted catastrophic structural destruction on NTPC's 520 MW Tapovan Vishnugad Hydropower Project, burying headrace tunnels in sludge and knocking out regional electricity supply.
        <br>&bull; <em>3. Destruction of Agriculture & Soil Erosion:</em> Violent flood surges stripped and eroded fertile river terrace soils, washing away standing winter crops and dumping tons of silt and rubble onto farm lands. Critical irrigation canals and riverside pastures were entirely destroyed.
        <br>&bull; <em>4. Disruption of Human Settlements & Economy:</em> More than 200 laborers, villagers, and personnel lost their lives or were declared missing. Riverbank dwellings and local market stalls were swept away, while pilgrimage tourism and trekking livelihoods suffered severe long-term setbacks.
        <br>&bull; <em>5. Public Health & Drinking Water:</em> Churning sediment heavily contaminated downstream drinking water supplies, forcing authorities to arrange emergency water shipments and portable filtration units for affected settlements.
    </li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Translate the given poster on landslide into your native language and display it in your home.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Landslide Safety Poster Guide (Ready Now to Stay Secure):</strong>
        <br>Below is the structured safety poster adapted for home display and regional reference, organized across the three critical disaster preparedness phases:
    </li>
</ul>

<div style="background: #fdfaf2; border: 2px solid #cfa670; border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin: 14px 0; font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; box-shadow: 0 3px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);">
    <div style="text-align: center; border-bottom: 2px solid #cfa670; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-bottom: 14px;">
        <h3 style="margin: 0; color: #a83232; font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.5px;">LANDSLIDE SAFETY ACTION PLAN</h3>
        <p style="margin: 4px 0 0 0; color: #555; font-size: 0.92rem; font-weight: 600;">Be Smart, Be Prepared | Ready Now to Stay Secure</p>
    </div>

    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 14px;">
        <!-- Before -->
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #e2d9cc; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; color: #b45309; font-size: 0.95rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #f0e6d6; padding-bottom: 4px;">1. Before (Preparedness)</h4>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;">
                <li><strong>Plant Deep-Rooted Trees:</strong> Grow vegetation and native trees that hold topsoil together firmly on slopes.</li>
                <li><strong>Monitor Weather Alerts:</strong> Listen to local radio, television, and official weather advisories for flash alerts.</li>
                <li><strong>Maintain Clear Drainage:</strong> Keep surface drains and weep holes clean to prevent water buildup behind retaining walls.</li>
                <li><strong>Watch for Warning Signs:</strong> Look out for ground subsidence, building tilting, cracks on rocks, or sudden muddying of streams.</li>
                <li><strong>Avoid Slope Construction:</strong> Never build homes or structures near steep, unstable hillsides or natural drainage paths.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- During -->
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #e2d9cc; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; color: #dc2626; font-size: 0.95rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #f0e6d6; padding-bottom: 4px;">2. During (Immediate Action)</h4>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;">
                <li><strong>Stay Calm & Avoid Panic:</strong> Remain composed and disregard unverified rumors or panic messages.</li>
                <li><strong>Stay Together:</strong> Keep your family and companions together in a secure, pre-identified safe zone.</li>
                <li><strong>Heed Warning Sounds:</strong> If you hear unusual noises like trees cracking or boulders tumbling, evacuate the slip path immediately.</li>
                <li><strong>Move to High Ground:</strong> Evacuate rapidly away from downstream valleys and gullies to stable, elevated ground.</li>
                <li><strong>Alert Authorities:</strong> Immediately inform the nearest Tehsil, police post, or District Disaster HQ.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- After -->
        <div style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #e2d9cc; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px;">
            <h4 style="margin: 0 0 8px 0; color: #15803d; font-size: 0.95rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #f0e6d6; padding-bottom: 4px;">3. After (Recovery & Safety)</h4>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;">
                <li><strong>Avoid Loose Debris & Wires:</strong> Never touch or walk over unstable slope debris, fallen electrical poles, or dangling power wires.</li>
                <li><strong>Maintain Safe Distance:</strong> Stay away from the landslide path and river channel, as secondary slips often follow.</li>
                <li><strong>Rescue & First Aid:</strong> Check for injured or trapped persons; administer first aid and do not move severely injured victims unless in danger.</li>
                <li><strong>Avoid Untreated Water:</strong> Do not drink contaminated water directly from silted rivers, springs, or wells; boil or filter first.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Helpline Footer -->
    <div style="background: #f4ede1; border-radius: 6px; padding: 10px 14px; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; font-size: 0.88rem;">
        <span style="color: #78350f; font-weight: 700;">📞 National Disaster Helpline (NDMA): <span style="color: #b91c1c; font-size: 1rem;">011-1078</span></span>
        <span style="color: #4b5563;">Official Portal: <strong>www.ndma.gov.in</strong></span>
    </div>
</div>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Home Display Instructions:</strong> Mount this safety card prominently on your household notice board, refrigerator, or near the main doorway alongside local emergency contacts for quick reference during heavy rains.</li>
</ul>`, image: `images/deez.png`
                        },
                        { 
                            q: "Divide the class into three groups. Each group will work on one project (water, wind, and glacier). The project should highlight the causes, impact on human life and the environment, and mitigation measures.", 
                            a: `<ul>
    <li><strong>Group 1 (Running Water & Waves):</strong> Examines river flooding, gully erosion, and coastal retreat; impacts include damaged arable land and lost property; mitigations include building embankments, check dams, and mangrove restoration.</li>
    <li><strong>Group 2 (Wind Action):</strong> Focuses on deflation, expanding sand dunes, and dust storms in arid tracts; impacts include topsoil desertification and respiratory hazards; mitigations include shelterbelt planting, sand fence stabilization, and sustainable grazing.</li>
    <li><strong>Group 3 (Glaciers & Ice):</strong> Covers avalanches and GLOFs driven by warming trends and moraine dam collapse; impacts include downstream flooding and infrastructure loss; mitigations include satellite lake monitoring, early warning sensors, and controlled siphon draining.</li>
</ul>` 
                        },
                    ]
                 },
                    { name: "Chapter 3: Atmosphere and Climate", solutions: [// Q1 & A1 (Includes high-visibility, large SVG breakdown of atmospheric gases)
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">\n  <p>1. What is atmosphere? Explain its composition with the help of a pie diagram.</p>\n</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">\n  <p>The atmosphere is a protective blanket of air surrounding the Earth, held down by gravitational pull. It shields life from harmful ultraviolet radiation and regulates surface temperatures.</p>\n  <div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px 0;">\n    <svg viewBox="0 0 760 380" width="100%" height="auto" style="max-width: 720px; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">\n      <!-- Main Pie: Center (200, 190), Radius = 145 -->\n      <!-- Nitrogen: 78% -->\n      <path d="M 200,190 L 200,45 A 145,145 0 1,1 60.1,215.1 Z" fill="#2e7d32"/>\n      <!-- Oxygen: 21% -->\n      <path d="M 200,190 L 60.1,215.1 A 145,145 0 0,1 190.9,45.3 Z" fill="#0288d1"/>\n      <!-- Combined Remaining Wedge: 1% -->\n      <path d="M 200,190 L 190.9,45.3 A 145,145 0 0,1 200,45 Z" fill="#d84315"/>\n\n      <!-- In-slice Primary Labels -->\n      <text x="245" y="225" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#ffffff">Nitrogen (78%)</text>\n      <text x="55" y="130" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="15" font-weight="bold" fill="#01579b">Oxygen (21%)</text>\n\n      <!-- Zoom Connector Lines to Expanded 1% Breakdown -->\n      <polygon points="191,45 200,45 420,50 420,330" fill="#fbe9e7" opacity="0.45"/>\n      <line x1="195" y1="45" x2="420" y2="50" stroke="#d84315" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,4"/>\n      <line x1="200" y1="190" x2="420" y2="330" stroke="#d84315" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,4"/>\n\n      <!-- Magnified Detail Panel for the 1% (Argon, CO2, Others) -->\n      <g transform="translate(420, 50)">\n        <!-- Header Bar -->\n        <rect width="310" height="34" rx="4" fill="#d84315"/>\n        <text x="155" y="22" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">DETAILED BREAKDOWN OF REMAINING 1%</text>\n        \n        <!-- Card Container -->\n        <rect y="34" width="310" height="246" fill="#fdfbf7" stroke="#d4c2a8" stroke-width="1.2" rx="0 0 4 4"/>\n        \n        <!-- Argon Slice Bar & Label -->\n        <rect x="20" y="55" width="18" height="18" fill="#f57c00" rx="3"/>\n        <text x="48" y="70" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#222">Argon (Ar): <tspan fill="#f57c00">0.93%</tspan></text>\n        <rect x="20" y="80" width="270" height="8" rx="4" fill="#ffe0b2"/>\n        <rect x="20" y="80" width="251" height="8" rx="4" fill="#f57c00"/>\n\n        <!-- Carbon Dioxide Slice Bar & Label -->\n        <rect x="20" y="115" width="18" height="18" fill="#c62828" rx="3"/>\n        <text x="48" y="130" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#222">Carbon Dioxide (CO₂): <tspan fill="#c62828">0.04%</tspan></text>\n        <rect x="20" y="140" width="270" height="8" rx="4" fill="#ffcdd2"/>\n        <rect x="20" y="140" width="11" height="8" rx="4" fill="#c62828"/>\n\n        <!-- Other Gases Combined Slice Bar & Label -->\n        <rect x="20" y="175" width="18" height="18" fill="#6a1b9a" rx="3"/>\n        <text x="48" y="190" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#222">Others Combined: <tspan fill="#6a1b9a">0.03%</tspan></text>\n        <text x="48" y="206" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10.5" fill="#666">Neon, Helium, Methane, Krypton, Ozone, H₂</text>\n        <rect x="20" y="216" width="270" height="8" rx="4" fill="#e1bee7"/>\n        <rect x="20" y="216" width="8" height="8" rx="4" fill="#6a1b9a"/>\n\n        <!-- Water Vapour Footnote -->\n        <line x1="20" y1="238" x2="290" y2="238" stroke="#e0d4c1" stroke-width="1"/>\n        <text x="20" y="258" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10.5" fill="#444">Water Vapour &amp; Dust: Variable (0.1% to 0.4%)</text>\n      </g>\n    </svg>\n  </div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Nitrogen (78%):</strong> The most abundant gas; essential for plant growth and living protein formation.</li>\n    <li><strong>Oxygen (21%):</strong> Critical for respiration in animals and humans, and supports combustion.</li>\n    <li><strong>Argon (0.93%):</strong> The most common noble and inert gas in atmospheric air.</li>\n    <li><strong>Carbon Dioxide (0.04%):</strong> Absorbed by plants during photosynthesis and retains warmth via the greenhouse effect.</li>\n    <li><strong>Other Elements Combined (0.03%):</strong> Minute quantities of neon, helium, methane, krypton, hydrogen, and ozone.</li>\n    <li><strong>Water Vapour &amp; Dust Particles:</strong> Range between 0.1% and 0.4%, responsible for condensation, cloud formation, and precipitation.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>`
  },

  // Q2 & A2
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">\n  <p>2. Draw a labelled diagram of the structure of atmosphere.</p>\n</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Troposphere (0 to 12 km):</strong> The lowest and densest layer where nearly all weather phenomena—clouds, rain, fog, and storms—occur. Temperature decreases with altitude.</li>\n    <li><strong>Stratosphere (12 to 50 km):</strong> Free of weather disturbances, making it ideal for flying jet aeroplanes. Houses the ozone layer which filters out harmful solar ultraviolet radiation.</li>\n    <li><strong>Mesosphere (50 to 80 km):</strong> The coldest atmospheric layer. Incoming meteorites burn up here due to friction with atmospheric gases.</li>\n    <li><strong>Thermosphere (80 to 700 km):</strong> Temperature rises rapidly with altitude as it absorbs solar X-rays. Contains the ionosphere which reflects radio transmissions back to Earth, and produces auroras.</li>\n    <li><strong>Exosphere (&gt; 700 km):</strong> The uppermost boundary where light gases like hydrogen and helium escape into space.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>`,
    image: `images/st.jpg`
  },

  // Q3 & A3
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">\n  <p>3. Which are the four main seasons of India?</p>\n</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Winter Season (Cold Weather):</strong> December to February; marked by low temperatures, clear skies, and calm weather across northern India.</li>\n    <li><strong>Summer Season (Pre-monsoon / Hot Weather):</strong> March to May; characterized by high temperatures, dry heat, and local dust storms.</li>\n    <li><strong>Advancing Monsoon Season (South-West Monsoon):</strong> June to September; brings the majority of India's annual rainfall from moisture-laden oceanic winds.</li>\n    <li><strong>Retreating Monsoon Season (Post-monsoon):</strong> October to November; skies clear across north India while moisture brings rainfall along the south-eastern coast.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>`
  },

  // Q4 & A4
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">\n  <p>4. Why do you not feel the pressure of the atmosphere?</p>\n</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Internal Counter-Pressure:</strong> The blood and internal fluids inside our bodies exert an outward pressure equal to the external air pressure.</li>\n    <li><strong>Omnidirectional Balance:</strong> Atmospheric pressure presses uniformly from all directions, canceling out the net force on our bodies.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>`
  },

  // Q5 & A5
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">\n  <p>5. In which layer of the atmosphere do aeroplanes fly and why?</p>\n</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Layer:</strong> Aeroplanes fly in the lower portion of the <strong>stratosphere</strong> (just above the tropopause).</li>\n    <li><strong>Reasons:</strong>\n      <br/>• <strong>Absence of Weather Turbulence:</strong> The stratosphere is free of clouds, convective storms, fog, and rain.\n      <br/>• <strong>Smooth Flight Conditions:</strong> Steady horizontal airflow ensures safety and fuel efficiency.\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n</div>`
  },

  // Q6 & A6
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">\n  <p>6. Distinguish between the following: a. The troposphere and stratosphere, b. The south-west monsoon and north-east monsoon.</p>\n</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">\n  <p><strong>a. Troposphere vs. Stratosphere:</strong></p>\n  <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 12px; font-size: 13px;">\n    <thead>\n      <tr style="background: #eedcb6; text-align: left;">\n        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Feature</th>\n        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Troposphere</th>\n        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Stratosphere</th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Altitude</strong></td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Extends up to an average height of 12 km.</td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Extends from 12 km up to 50 km.</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Temperature</strong></td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Decreases steadily as altitude increases.</td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Increases with altitude due to ozone absorption of UV rays.</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Weather</strong></td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Site of almost all active weather phenomena (rain, clouds, storms).</td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Dry, cloudless, and calm; ideal for aircraft.</td>\n      </tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n\n  <p><strong>b. South-West Monsoon vs. North-East Monsoon:</strong></p>\n  <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 13px;">\n    <thead>\n      <tr style="background: #eedcb6; text-align: left;">\n        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Feature</th>\n        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">South-West Monsoon</th>\n        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">North-East Monsoon</th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Active Season</strong></td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">June to September (summer season).</td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">October to February (winter season).</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Direction of Wind</strong></td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Blows from oceanic waters toward landmass (sea to land).</td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Blows from land outward toward the oceans (land to sea).</td>\n      </tr>\n      <tr>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Rainfall Impact</strong></td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Provides over 75% of total annual rainfall across India.</td>\n        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Largely dry; brings vital winter rains to Tamil Nadu and parts of Andhra Pradesh via Bay of Bengal moisture.</td>\n      </tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>`
  },

  // Q7 & A7
  {
  q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>7. Do it yourself: Table 3.3 shows average monthly temperatures and rainfall for 10 representative stations. Study these figures on your own and convert them into 'temperature and rainfall' graphs. (One such graph for Delhi, Fig. 3.14, is prepared below). Answer questions 7.1 to 7.5 based on the data.</p>
</div>`,
  a: `<div class="a-block">
  <div style="text-align: center; margin: 16px 0;">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 360" width="100%" height="auto" style="max-width: 540px; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8; border-radius: 8px;">
      <!-- Title -->
      <text x="300" y="26" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#333" text-anchor="middle">Fig. 3.14: Temperature and Rainfall of Delhi</text>
      
      <!-- Axis Lines & Grids -->
      <!-- Left Y Axis (Temperature 0 to 35 C), Right Y Axis (Rainfall 0 to 20 cm) -->
      <!-- Origin: (70, 290), Width: 460, Height: 240. 35 C = 240px -> 6.85px per C. 20 cm = 240px -> 12px per cm -->
      <line x1="70" y1="290" x2="530" y2="290" stroke="#888" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <line x1="70" y1="50" x2="70" y2="290" stroke="#888" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <line x1="530" y1="50" x2="530" y2="290" stroke="#888" stroke-width="1.5"/>

      <!-- Grid lines horizontal -->
      <line x1="70" y1="230" x2="530" y2="230" stroke="#eee" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="70" y1="170" x2="530" y2="170" stroke="#eee" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="70" y1="110" x2="530" y2="110" stroke="#eee" stroke-width="1"/>
      <line x1="70" y1="50" x2="530" y2="50" stroke="#eee" stroke-width="1"/>

      <!-- Left Y Axis Labels (Temp °C) -->
      <text x="60" y="294" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#c62828" text-anchor="end">0°</text>
      <text x="60" y="224" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#c62828" text-anchor="end">10°</text>
      <text x="60" y="154" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#c62828" text-anchor="end">20°</text>
      <text x="60" y="84" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#c62828" text-anchor="end">30°</text>
      <text x="60" y="54" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#c62828" text-anchor="end">35°C</text>

      <!-- Right Y Axis Labels (Rainfall cm) -->
      <text x="540" y="294" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#0288d1">0</text>
      <text x="540" y="234" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#0288d1">5</text>
      <text x="540" y="174" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#0288d1">10</text>
      <text x="540" y="114" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#0288d1">15</text>
      <text x="540" y="54" font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#0288d1">20 cm</text>

      <!-- Monthly Bars (Rainfall) Center positions spaced across 460px: dx = 38px -->
      <!-- Jan (2.5): h=30 -->  <rect x="79" y="260" width="20" height="30" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Feb (1.5): h=18 -->  <rect x="117" y="272" width="20" height="18" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Mar (1.3): h=15 -->  <rect x="155" y="275" width="20" height="15" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Apr (1.0): h=12 -->  <rect x="193" y="278" width="20" height="12" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- May (1.8): h=21 -->  <rect x="231" y="269" width="20" height="21" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Jun (7.4): h=88 -->  <rect x="269" y="202" width="20" height="88" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Jul (19.3): h=231 --><rect x="307" y="59" width="20" height="231" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Aug (17.8): h=213 --><rect x="345" y="77" width="20" height="213" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Sep (11.9): h=142 --><rect x="383" y="148" width="20" height="142" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Oct (1.3): h=15 -->  <rect x="421" y="275" width="20" height="15" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Nov (0.2): h=3 -->   <rect x="459" y="287" width="20" height="3" fill="#0288d1"/>
      <!-- Dec (1.0): h=12 -->  <rect x="497" y="278" width="20" height="12" fill="#0288d1"/>

      <!-- Monthly Temperature Points & Polyline (Red line) -->
      <!-- Points: (x, 290 - temp * 6.85) -->
      <!-- Jan:14.4 (89, 191), Feb:16.7 (127, 175), Mar:23.3 (165, 130), Apr:30.0 (203, 84), May:33.3 (241, 62), Jun:33.3 (279, 62) -->
      <!-- Jul:30.0 (317, 84), Aug:29.4 (355, 88), Sep:28.9 (393, 92), Oct:25.6 (431, 114), Nov:19.4 (469, 157), Dec:15.6 (507, 183) -->
      <polyline points="89,191 127,175 165,130 203,84 241,62 279,62 317,84 355,88 393,92 431,114 469,157 507,183" fill="none" stroke="#d32f2f" stroke-width="2.5"/>
      
      <circle cx="89" cy="191" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="127" cy="175" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="165" cy="130" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="203" cy="84" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="241" cy="62" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="279" cy="62" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="317" cy="84" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="355" cy="88" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="393" cy="92" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="431" cy="114" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="469" cy="157" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>
      <circle cx="507" cy="183" r="3.5" fill="#d32f2f"/>

      <!-- Month Labels X Axis -->
      <g font-family="Arial" font-size="10" fill="#444" text-anchor="middle">
        <text x="89" y="306">Jan</text>
        <text x="127" y="306">Feb</text>
        <text x="165" y="306">Mar</text>
        <text x="203" y="306">Apr</text>
        <text x="241" y="306">May</text>
        <text x="279" y="306">Jun</text>
        <text x="317" y="306">Jul</text>
        <text x="355" y="306">Aug</text>
        <text x="393" y="306">Sep</text>
        <text x="431" y="306">Oct</text>
        <text x="469" y="306">Nov</text>
        <text x="507" y="306">Dec</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Legend -->
      <g transform="translate(180, 332)" font-family="Arial" font-size="11">
        <line x1="0" y1="6" x2="24" y2="6" stroke="#d32f2f" stroke-width="2.5"/>
        <circle cx="12" cy="6" r="3" fill="#d32f2f"/>
        <text x="30" y="10" fill="#333">Temperature (°C)</text>
        <rect x="140" y="0" width="14" height="12" fill="#0288d1"/>
        <text x="160" y="10" fill="#333">Rainfall (cm)</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <p><strong>7.1 Stations Re-arranged According to Distance from Equator (Lowest to Highest Latitude):</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Thiruvananthapuram (8°29′N)</li>
    <li>Bengaluru (12°58′N)</li>
    <li>Chennai (13°4′N)</li>
    <li>Mumbai (19°N)</li>
    <li>Nagpur (21°9′N)</li>
    <li>Kolkata (22°34′N)</li>
    <li>Shillong (24°34′N)</li>
    <li>Jodhpur (26°18′N)</li>
    <li>Delhi (29°N)</li>
    <li>Leh (34°N)</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>7.2 Climatic Extremes and Influences:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>a. Two stations with the most extreme climate:</strong> Leh (sub-zero winter temperatures, high altitude) and Delhi / Jodhpur (high annual temperature range between hot summers and chilly winters).</li>
    <li><strong>b. Two stations influenced by retreating / winter monsoons:</strong> Chennai and Thiruvananthapuram.</li>
    <li><strong>c. The two hottest stations:</strong>
      <br/>• <em>In February:</em> Thiruvananthapuram (28.7°C) and Chennai (25.7°C).
      <br/>• <em>In June:</em> Jodhpur (33.9°C) and Delhi (33.3°C).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>7.3 Geographic Reasons for Rainfall Variations:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>a. Shillong vs. Kolkata:</strong> Shillong sits in the Khasi Hills where moisture-laden Bay of Bengal monsoon winds are funneled and forced upward, causing heavy orographic precipitation compared to low-lying Kolkata.</li>
    <li><strong>b. Delhi vs. Jodhpur:</strong> Delhi lies further east along the path of the monsoon trough, while Jodhpur lies in western Rajasthan where monsoon winds have shed most moisture and the Aravalli range aligns parallel to winds without obstructing them.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>7.4 Climate Explanations:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>a. Thiruvananthapuram's equable climate:</strong> Its coastal location close to the equator grants strong moderating maritime influences with negligible seasonal temperature variation.</li>
    <li><strong>b. Chennai's late rainfall:</strong> Chennai lies on the Coromandel coast, positioned in the rain-shadow of the Western Ghats during the south-west monsoon; it receives the majority of its rain between October and December from north-east retreating winds picking up Bay of Bengal moisture.</li>
    <li><strong>c. Leh's moderate precipitation:</strong> Leh is located on an elevated plateau enclosed by the Great Himalayas, which block monsoon winds, leaving it in a cold arid rain-shadow zone.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>7.5 Monsoon as a Unifying Framework:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Despite thermal and regional variations, the rhythmic seasonal cycle of the monsoons regulates agricultural rhythms, cultural celebrations, water replenishment, and river systems throughout the entire country.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
},

  // Q8 & A8
  {
  q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>8. Collect pictures of houses and clothing of people from different regions of India. Examine whether they reflect any relationship with the climatic conditions or the relief of those regions.</p>
</div>`,
  a: `<div class="a-block">
  <div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 16px 0;">
    <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 240px; max-width: 300px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px; background: #fff;">
      <img src="images/ladakh_house.jpg" alt="Traditional stone house in Ladakh with flat roof" style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px;" />
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px; font-weight: bold; color: #5d4037;">Ladakh: Stone &amp; Mud-Brick House</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 240px; max-width: 300px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px; background: #fff;">
      <img src="images/ladakh_clothing.png" alt="People in Ladakh wearing thick woollen goncha" style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 6px;" />
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 13px; font-weight: bold; color: #5d4037;">Ladakh: Traditional Woollen Goncha</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chosen Region:</strong> Ladakh (Cold High-Altitude Desert).</li>
    <li><strong>Housing vs. Climate and Relief:</strong>
      <br/>• Houses are built with thick two-storey stone and sun-dried mud-brick walls to trap heat and insulate living areas from sub-zero winter temperatures.
      <br/>• Roofs are completely flat because Ladakh lies in a rain-shadow zone with minimal rainfall or snowfall; the flat roofs serve as drying platforms for winter food supplies.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Clothing vs. Climate:</strong>
      <br/>• People wear the <em>goncha</em> (a thick, warm woollen robe tied at the waist) made from local sheep and yak wool.
      <br/>• Heavy woollen caps and boots insulate against extreme freezing winds and prevent frostbite.
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>`
},
] },
                     ]
            },
            {   
                name: "Part 1 - History",     
                chapters: [
                { name: `Chapter 4: Early Humans and Beginning
of Civilisation
` , solutions: [// Q1 & A1
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>1. Do you think life became easier or more challenging after humans started farming? Give two reasons for your answer.</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Life Became More Secure and Predictable:</strong> Farming ensured a reliable, localized supply of food through cultivated cereals and domesticated livestock, ending the constant hazard and physical exhaustion of roaming to hunt wild game.</li>
    <li><strong>Life Brought New Demands and Vulnerabilities:</strong> Agriculture demanded rigorous year-round manual labor—clearing forests, tilling soil, weeding, and managing irrigation—while making communities vulnerable to famine if seasonal rainfall failed or pestilence wiped out stored crops.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q2 & A2
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>2. The environment offers human societies both opportunities as well as challenges. Explain with reference to early farming communities and river-valley civilisations.</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Environmental Opportunities:</strong> River valleys like the Indus, Nile, Tigris-Euphrates, and Huang He provided perpetual supplies of freshwater, naturally fertile alluvial silt renewed by seasonal floods, and navigable waterways for transport and trade.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental Challenges:</strong> Unpredictable, violent flooding could submerge entire settlements, prolonged droughts dried out field crops, and soil exhaustion or marshy diseases threatened community survival, prompting the construction of check dams, canals, and embankments.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q3 & A3
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>3. Why do historians divide early human history into different ages such as Stone Age, Bronze Age, and Iron Age? What does this classification tell us about human progress?</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reason for Periodisation:</strong> The classification is based on the primary raw material used by humans to manufacture their essential tools, implements, and weapons during that historical era.</li>
    <li><strong>Significance for Human Progress:</strong> It illustrates humanity's increasing technological mastery over nature—moving from chipping raw stones to smelting metal alloys like bronze, and eventually extracting durable iron, which allowed deeper ploughing, dense forest clearance, and the growth of complex urban civilizations.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q4 & A4
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>4. Imagine you are a Neolithic farmer. Describe one day of your life. What challenges would you face that a hunter-gatherer would not?</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A Day in the Life:</strong> Waking up in a sun-dried mud-brick hut, tending to domesticated goats and cattle, weeding the barley plots using polished stone hoes, processing grain with querns, and firing clay pots for storing evening meals.</li>
    <li><strong>Unique Agricultural Challenges:</strong> Unlike mobile hunter-gatherers who could follow wildlife when conditions changed, a farmer was bound to one plot of land (sedentism), risking total starvation if pests, drought, or floods ruined the crop.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q5 & A5
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>5. Imagine that the Harappan script gets deciphered tomorrow. What new types of information do you think historians might learn?</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Political and Administrative Structures:</strong> Decipherment would reveal whether the Indus cities were ruled by monarchies, priest-kings, or merchant councils, and explain the administrative rules behind their uniform town planning.</li>
    <li><strong>Religious Beliefs and Literature:</strong> It would provide direct knowledge of the names of their gods, the purpose of rituals on seals, their mythologies, and specific diplomatic or trade records with West Asian kingdoms like Sumer and Akkad.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q6 & A6
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>6. Prepare a table with three columns—Palaeolithic, Mesolithic, and Neolithic and fill in their distinctive features: tools, settlements, art, and subsistence.</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 13px;">
    <thead>
      <tr style="background: #eedcb6; text-align: left;">
        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Feature</th>
        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Palaeolithic</th>
        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Mesolithic</th>
        <th style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Neolithic</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Tools</strong></td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Large stone handaxes, cleavers, choppers, scrapers</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Microliths (tiny composite stone points, arrowheads)</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Polished stone axes, celts, grinding querns</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Settlements</strong></td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Nomadic shelters, natural caves, open river camps</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Temporary seasonal camps, rock shelters</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Permanent village homes of mud and sun-dried brick</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Art</strong></td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Basic body pigments, shell and bone beads</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Dynamic rock paintings (e.g., Bhimbetka caves)</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Painted pottery, terracotta figures, decorative beads</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;"><strong>Subsistence</strong></td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Hunting large wild animals, gathering roots and berries</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Hunting small game, fishing, wild grain gathering</td>
        <td style="padding: 6px; border: 1px solid #d4c2a8;">Cultivating crops, herding domesticated livestock</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>`
  },

  // Q7 & A7
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>7. "Bronze Age civilisations developed independently but shared common features." Examine this statement with reference to the civilisations given in the chapter.</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Independent River Valley Origins:</strong> Civilisations arose in distinct geographical river basins—Sindhu-Sarasvatī along the Indus and Sarasvatī, Mesopotamia along the Euphrates and Tigris, Egypt along the Nile, and China along the Huang He and Yangtze.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared Urban and Economic Features:</strong> Each relied on fertile river alluvium for high-yield agriculture, mastered bronze and copper metallurgy, established organized city planning, used standardized weights or measures, and developed early writing systems (like cuneiform, hieroglyphs, or oracle bone script) to regulate administration and trade.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q8 & A8
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>8. Although rivers provided many benefits, they also created challenges for early societies. Discuss both the advantages and disadvantages of settling near rivers.</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Advantages of River Settlements:</strong> Rivers provided reliable year-round freshwater, deposited fertile silt (such as kemet in Egypt) during seasonal floods, supported fishing, and served as vital highways for long-distance boat navigation and trade.</li>
    <li><strong>Disadvantages and Hazards:</strong> Unpredictable, violent flooding could wash away homes and fields, seasonal drought caused water shortages, shifting river courses left cities stranded, and stagnant waters bred diseases.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q9 & A9
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>9. With the help of your teacher, find out more about the Code of Hammurabi. Why was it important? Do you think it was fair to all sections of society? Give reasons for your answer.</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Importance of the Code:</strong> Compiled by King Hammurabi around 1792 BCE in Babylon, it provided a written set of uniform civil and criminal laws inscribed on stone stelae, establishing accountability and serving as a model for future legal systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Fairness Evaluation:</strong> It was not equally fair to all social classes. While it offered protections against fraud and malpractice, punishments strictly depended on social hierarchy, imposing harsher physical penalties when crimes were committed against upper-class elites compared to lower-class commoners or enslaved people.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q10 & A10
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>10. If you had to choose one major innovation from early civilisations that changed the world permanently, what would it be and why?</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Major Innovation:</strong> The Invention of Writing Systems (such as Sumerian cuneiform, Egyptian hieroglyphs, and Harappan script).</li>
    <li><strong>Global Impact:</strong> Writing allowed humans to permanently record knowledge, trade transactions, laws, scientific calculations, and history across generations, moving humanity from prehistory into recorded civilization.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q11 & A11
  {
    q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>11. Compare the social hierarchy and daily life of people in the Egyptian civilisation with those in Mesopotamia or China. What similarities and differences do you notice?</p>
</div>`,
    a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Similarities:</strong> All three civilisations were deeply stratified with a divine or sacred monarch at the peak (Pharaoh, Mesopotamian Priest-King, or Zhou King with the Mandate of Heaven), supported by nobles/priests, while peasants, artisans, and laborers formed the broad base.</li>
    <li><strong>Differences:</strong> Egyptian society placed massive focus on funerary rituals and the afterlife (mummification, building mastabas and pyramids), while Mesopotamia centered public life around city-state temple complexes (ziggurats), and early Chinese society emphasized divination using oracle bones and ancestral bronze rituals. Furthermore, Egyptian women held legal rights to own property and run businesses.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
  },

  // Q12 & A12 (STRICTLY REQUIRES IMAGE / MAP OF TRADE LINKS)
  {
  q: `<div class="q-block">
  <p>12. Activity: Using maps, locate the major rivers and civilisations of Mesopotamia, Egypt, China, and the Sindhu-Sarasvatī Valley. Mark the trade links between them.</p>
</div>`,
  a: `<div class="a-block">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Major Rivers and Civilisations:</strong> Nile River in Egypt, Tigris and Euphrates in Mesopotamia, Indus and Sarasvatī in northwestern India, and the Huang He and Yangtze in China.</li>
    <li><strong>Documented Trade Links:</strong> Archaeological and cuneiform records verify regular sea and coastal trade between the Harappan Civilisation (known as Meluhha) and Mesopotamia via intermediary gulf ports of Dilmun (Bahrain) and Magan (Oman), exchanging copper, carnelian beads, ivory, timber, and lapis lazuli   .</li>
  </ul>
</div>`, image: `images/hii.jpg`
},

  // Q13 & A13 (STRICTLY REQUIRES IMAGE / PRESENTATION SCRAPBOOK VISUAL)
  {
